WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.380
&gt;&gt; Well, good afternoon
everybody, thanks for coming today

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.380
I appreciate it. I appreciate
everyone's patience today.

00:00:11.381 --> 00:00:14.210
One of the things
I wanted to mention

00:00:14.211 --> 00:00:16.985
that really resonated with
me was the following quote,

00:00:16.986 --> 00:00:19.485
"Failure from small mistakes to

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:23.040
huge miscalculations is
a necessary and critical part

00:00:23.041 --> 00:00:24.750
of our children's development."

00:00:24.751 --> 00:00:26.970
These words really
resonated well with me at

00:00:26.971 --> 00:00:29.675
the time when I first read them,

00:00:29.676 --> 00:00:32.070
because it was also
about the time we were

00:00:32.071 --> 00:00:33.155
going through our own growth

00:00:33.156 --> 00:00:34.990
mindset effort here
at the company,

00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:36.960
and I was dealing with
the terrible teens

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:38.415
and my own kids at home.

00:00:38.416 --> 00:00:40.420
And so trying to be
able to figure out

00:00:40.421 --> 00:00:42.595
when should I let
my son miss the bus,

00:00:42.596 --> 00:00:44.900
how many times should
I let him miss the bus,

00:00:44.901 --> 00:00:46.360
and then what do we
need to do to really

00:00:46.361 --> 00:00:47.730
make sure that he's
going to grow up to be

00:00:47.731 --> 00:00:51.515
a successful child and
a successful adult.

00:00:51.516 --> 00:00:54.180
Our speakers today, her book

00:00:54.181 --> 00:00:56.530
really helped me and
my wife in this effort.

00:00:56.531 --> 00:00:58.800
I'd like to welcome
educator, writer,

00:00:58.801 --> 00:01:01.095
and speaker,
Jessica Lahey today.

00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:05.450
As an English writer,
English and writing teacher,

00:01:05.451 --> 00:01:07.450
a columnist for
the New York Times,

00:01:07.451 --> 00:01:09.010
and corresponded
with The Atlantic,

00:01:09.011 --> 00:01:10.120
which I have a subscription,

00:01:10.121 --> 00:01:11.850
I recommend you
all should as well.

00:01:11.851 --> 00:01:14.225
She's also author of
the best-selling book,

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:15.570
The Gift of Failure.

00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:17.700
How the best parents learn

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:19.980
and let go so their
children can succeed.

00:01:19.981 --> 00:01:21.890
I'm hopeful that I can
be one of those parents

00:01:21.891 --> 00:01:23.480
in some lifetime at this point,

00:01:23.481 --> 00:01:24.770
but I'd like to introduce Jess,

00:01:24.771 --> 00:01:26.420
and welcome here
at Microsoft today.

00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:30.675
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:01:30.676 --> 00:01:32.200
Thank you so so much
for coming today,

00:01:32.201 --> 00:01:34.220
I understand there's
competing talks today,

00:01:34.221 --> 00:01:35.700
so I really really appreciate

00:01:35.701 --> 00:01:37.830
the attendance and
today I'm going to be

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.425
getting into some of the details

00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:41.880
of sort of how I ended
up here and my work,

00:01:41.881 --> 00:01:43.660
but one of the things you
should keep in mind is that

00:01:43.661 --> 00:01:45.660
my son Finnegan, if
you've read my book,

00:01:45.661 --> 00:01:48.440
my son Fin is sort of
a centerpiece of the book,

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:51.200
and he is here today.

00:01:51.201 --> 00:01:54.130
So and he's happy to answer
questions occasionally.

00:01:54.131 --> 00:01:57.230
So he's also acting
as my assistant today.

00:01:57.231 --> 00:01:59.755
So anyway why I'm here,

00:01:59.756 --> 00:02:01.865
so I have been
a teacher for 20 years,

00:02:01.866 --> 00:02:04.395
I've taught every grade
from six to 12th grade,

00:02:04.396 --> 00:02:07.330
I was a Latin and English
and writing teacher,

00:02:07.331 --> 00:02:10.380
and currently I'm
an English teacher in

00:02:10.381 --> 00:02:14.575
an in-patient drug and alcohol
rehab for kids in Vermont.

00:02:14.576 --> 00:02:16.990
So my work is actually heading

00:02:16.991 --> 00:02:19.990
more in the direction
of addiction prevention.

00:02:19.991 --> 00:02:21.950
That's what
my next book is about,

00:02:21.951 --> 00:02:24.225
preventing addiction
in adolescence

00:02:24.226 --> 00:02:26.450
by starting when
they're very very small,

00:02:26.451 --> 00:02:28.390
and so that's what
I'm really excited,

00:02:28.391 --> 00:02:31.590
to sort of segue into
those conversations.

00:02:31.591 --> 00:02:34.310
But what this book
really has been about for

00:02:34.311 --> 00:02:37.245
me is so after
20 years of teaching,

00:02:37.246 --> 00:02:39.280
I think when I first started,

00:02:39.281 --> 00:02:41.820
and by the way I never
intended to be a teacher.

00:02:41.821 --> 00:02:44.110
I thought I was going to
be a juvenile attorney.

00:02:44.111 --> 00:02:45.720
I went to law school and studied

00:02:45.721 --> 00:02:47.200
Education law and Juvenile law,

00:02:47.201 --> 00:02:49.375
I was going to work
in Juvenile court,

00:02:49.376 --> 00:02:52.880
and then I was asked
to go teach a class,

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:54.760
and I came home that first day

00:02:54.761 --> 00:02:56.470
and my husband took
one look at me and he

00:02:56.471 --> 00:03:00.020
said "Are you even going
to finish law school?"

00:03:00.021 --> 00:03:01.420
Because it was so
obvious that I was

00:03:01.421 --> 00:03:03.100
completely sunk as a teacher,

00:03:03.101 --> 00:03:04.520
as that was what
I was going to be,

00:03:04.521 --> 00:03:06.085
and that's what I've
been ever since.

00:03:06.086 --> 00:03:07.510
So I finished law school but I

00:03:07.511 --> 00:03:08.770
went straight into teaching,

00:03:08.771 --> 00:03:11.265
and have been doing that
my entire adult career.

00:03:11.266 --> 00:03:13.315
And most of my career actually,

00:03:13.316 --> 00:03:15.760
my heart lies with
middle school.

00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:19.990
Because middle school was
this incredible place where

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:26.680
my job is to watch kids
screw up all day long,

00:03:26.681 --> 00:03:28.200
and I get to especially where

00:03:28.201 --> 00:03:29.760
I've most recently
taught middle school,

00:03:29.761 --> 00:03:31.580
it was really really small,

00:03:31.581 --> 00:03:34.830
our school area, our classrooms
and our locker room,

00:03:34.831 --> 00:03:36.580
so my office was sort

00:03:36.581 --> 00:03:38.650
of right outside one
of the locker rooms,

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.970
and so I would hear
these kids out there talking,

00:03:40.971 --> 00:03:42.895
and I would go out there
and mill about with them,

00:03:42.896 --> 00:03:47.400
and I'd say something
like "So sweetie,

00:03:47.401 --> 00:03:54.030
today is the sixth day
you've forgotten everything.

00:03:54.031 --> 00:03:56.260
So what are you going to
do, what are we going to do,

00:03:56.261 --> 00:04:00.390
how can I help you come up
with a strategy for day seven,

00:04:00.391 --> 00:04:03.305
what's your plan for day seven?"

00:04:03.306 --> 00:04:04.900
And we get to talking about

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:06.780
some possible ways to
use this plan book,

00:04:06.781 --> 00:04:08.495
how he's using it now,

00:04:08.496 --> 00:04:11.670
if he's even using
his plan book by the way,

00:04:11.671 --> 00:04:12.880
what we can do differently,

00:04:12.881 --> 00:04:14.010
maybe for day seven.

00:04:14.011 --> 00:04:17.610
And what started to happen
more and more often,

00:04:17.611 --> 00:04:18.970
was we get into

00:04:18.971 --> 00:04:21.345
this great learning
opportunity and he was

00:04:21.346 --> 00:04:23.550
listening and I was helping him

00:04:23.551 --> 00:04:25.120
through the things he tried

00:04:25.121 --> 00:04:26.390
and things that hadn't worked,

00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:28.140
and let's try this instead,

00:04:28.141 --> 00:04:31.140
and then all of a sudden
about 10 years ago,

00:04:31.141 --> 00:04:34.660
what started to happen was
the door would open and

00:04:34.661 --> 00:04:36.920
the parent would run
through the door with

00:04:36.921 --> 00:04:39.520
like "Here's your
homework, here it is."

00:04:39.521 --> 00:04:43.860
And the kid would say
"Well, that's my strategy,

00:04:43.861 --> 00:04:45.730
I mean why do I need to come up

00:04:45.731 --> 00:04:47.790
with anything else
because all I have

00:04:47.791 --> 00:04:49.710
to do is text my parents

00:04:49.711 --> 00:04:52.315
and they'll bring
it to me at school".

00:04:52.316 --> 00:04:55.780
And from my perspective
that was frustrating.

00:04:55.781 --> 00:04:57.490
And that sort of
was the beginning of

00:04:57.491 --> 00:04:58.905
what I also started to notice

00:04:58.906 --> 00:05:02.650
as a real divide between
teachers and parents.

00:05:02.651 --> 00:05:07.200
When I first started
teaching I sort of thought,

00:05:07.201 --> 00:05:09.550
"It's going to be beautiful,
it's going to be me,

00:05:09.551 --> 00:05:11.570
and the parents, and the kids,

00:05:11.571 --> 00:05:14.780
and it's going to be
all about the education,

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.950
and we'll braid
each other's hair,

00:05:16.951 --> 00:05:18.570
and talk about the learning."

00:05:18.571 --> 00:05:21.040
And that's not where
we are right now,

00:05:21.041 --> 00:05:24.625
especially in really high
anxiety, high pressure schools.

00:05:24.626 --> 00:05:27.700
Increasingly we have
this adversarial relationship

00:05:27.701 --> 00:05:30.520
between parents and teachers,

00:05:30.521 --> 00:05:34.575
which as a teacher
that just doesn't work,

00:05:34.576 --> 00:05:37.530
because there's plenty
of research to show

00:05:37.531 --> 00:05:41.005
that the better the relationship
between home and school,

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:43.465
the more productive
the kid can be,

00:05:43.466 --> 00:05:45.340
the better informed
the parents feel,

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:47.085
and the better the kid does.

00:05:47.086 --> 00:05:48.680
So, creating a schism between

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:50.860
home and school benefits no one.

00:05:50.861 --> 00:05:53.505
And so then I was
concerned about that,

00:05:53.506 --> 00:05:58.280
and then I became acquainted
with Dan Pink's work.

00:05:58.281 --> 00:06:01.900
Obviously it's out there
in terms of his TedTalk,

00:06:01.901 --> 00:06:03.265
and his book Drive,

00:06:03.266 --> 00:06:06.630
and knew that a lot
of the things we were

00:06:06.631 --> 00:06:11.010
doing to get kids to engage,

00:06:11.011 --> 00:06:13.790
whether that's behaviorally
or with their schoolwork,

00:06:13.791 --> 00:06:15.220
things like sticker charts,

00:06:15.221 --> 00:06:18.540
and things like money for
grades and stuff like that,

00:06:18.541 --> 00:06:20.190
I knew from reading

00:06:20.191 --> 00:06:21.600
Dan Pink's work and the work of

00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:23.465
someone else that I'll
talk about in a minute,

00:06:23.466 --> 00:06:25.335
that that doesn't work.

00:06:25.336 --> 00:06:27.820
That it feels like it works

00:06:27.821 --> 00:06:30.040
because it works
in the short-term,

00:06:30.041 --> 00:06:31.460
but over the long-term we

00:06:31.461 --> 00:06:33.705
undermine kids
motivation to learn.

00:06:33.706 --> 00:06:36.430
So that sort of
really well-trod territory

00:06:36.431 --> 00:06:38.425
and I'll go into
the research in a second,

00:06:38.426 --> 00:06:41.510
but what was even
more concerning was

00:06:41.511 --> 00:06:45.500
that I was starting to
feel, and I wasn't sure,

00:06:45.501 --> 00:06:48.040
this is pre-writing the book
and doing all the research,

00:06:48.041 --> 00:06:51.950
that not only were we
undermining kids motivation,

00:06:51.951 --> 00:06:57.000
we were undermining
their actual ability to learn.

00:06:57.001 --> 00:06:58.550
That when we intervene,

00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:00.445
when we run through the door
with their homework,

00:07:00.446 --> 00:07:02.820
when we offer money for grades,

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:04.860
when we use sticker
charts for kids,

00:07:04.861 --> 00:07:07.000
when we rely on the grade

00:07:07.001 --> 00:07:09.810
itself as the reward
as I'll get into,

00:07:09.811 --> 00:07:12.600
that what we actually
do is make it so that

00:07:12.601 --> 00:07:15.450
our kids are less able to learn,

00:07:15.451 --> 00:07:17.460
they are less teachable,

00:07:17.461 --> 00:07:19.740
and that was devastating.

00:07:19.741 --> 00:07:21.810
I had a student who
wrote a paper for me

00:07:21.811 --> 00:07:24.550
explaining that
her obsession with being

00:07:24.551 --> 00:07:27.470
perfect had made
her so obsessed with

00:07:27.471 --> 00:07:29.055
her grades and her scores

00:07:29.056 --> 00:07:31.000
and looking good in front of me,

00:07:31.001 --> 00:07:33.130
and looking good in front
of her classmates that

00:07:33.131 --> 00:07:35.850
she had lost
interest in learning.

00:07:35.851 --> 00:07:37.650
It was no longer
about that for her.

00:07:37.651 --> 00:07:39.680
She was so obsessed with
the points and the grades

00:07:39.681 --> 00:07:42.450
that learning was
now as she said,

00:07:42.451 --> 00:07:45.290
beside the point which
again devastating for me,

00:07:45.291 --> 00:07:46.990
because I taught
her for three years.

00:07:46.991 --> 00:07:49.830
I was her advisor,
and at the time,

00:07:49.831 --> 00:07:51.120
hold your hats here,

00:07:51.121 --> 00:07:52.320
because at the time
I was teaching

00:07:52.321 --> 00:07:54.260
Latin and English and writing.

00:07:54.261 --> 00:07:55.960
Sixth grade, Latin.
Seventh grade,

00:07:55.961 --> 00:07:57.010
Latin. Eighth grade, Latin.

00:07:57.011 --> 00:07:58.690
Seventh grade, writing.
Eighth grade, writing.

00:07:58.691 --> 00:08:00.450
Seventh grade, English,
and eighth grade English,

00:08:00.451 --> 00:08:03.130
all at the same time
and I was her advisor.

00:08:03.131 --> 00:08:04.960
So there were days
where I had her for

00:08:04.961 --> 00:08:07.220
three classes and
an advisory period,

00:08:07.221 --> 00:08:09.095
so I knew her really well.

00:08:09.096 --> 00:08:10.650
And this is a girl
who had come into

00:08:10.651 --> 00:08:12.220
my classroom in sixth grade

00:08:12.221 --> 00:08:16.100
just so excited to
learn, she couldn't wait.

00:08:16.101 --> 00:08:17.650
"Mrs. Lahey, Mrs. Lahey,

00:08:17.651 --> 00:08:18.880
what are we going
to learn today?"

00:08:18.881 --> 00:08:20.530
And now she's telling me in

00:08:20.531 --> 00:08:23.290
eighth grade that she no
longer cares about learning.

00:08:23.291 --> 00:08:25.515
So, somewhere along
the way we screwed her up,

00:08:25.516 --> 00:08:26.910
we did that to her.

00:08:26.911 --> 00:08:28.630
Between and first of course,

00:08:28.631 --> 00:08:31.160
from my very high horse
as a teacher,

00:08:31.161 --> 00:08:34.215
I was like "Her parents
have done that to her,

00:08:34.216 --> 00:08:36.675
it's not me, I'm fantastic.

00:08:36.676 --> 00:08:41.010
But her parents are screwing
up my ability to teach her

00:08:41.011 --> 00:08:42.430
in the way this is me and

00:08:42.431 --> 00:08:45.235
my noble profession and
education and learning,

00:08:45.236 --> 00:08:49.465
and they, you people are
screwing it up for me."

00:08:49.466 --> 00:08:53.640
Unfortunately, then I had
these children of my own,

00:08:53.641 --> 00:08:56.395
I have one who's a freshman
in college right now,

00:08:56.396 --> 00:08:59.205
and Finnegan, Fin who
is in eighth grade,

00:08:59.206 --> 00:09:00.885
thank you I saw that look,

00:09:00.886 --> 00:09:05.260
and at the time I realized

00:09:05.261 --> 00:09:07.880
as angry as I wanted to be

00:09:07.881 --> 00:09:11.040
at you people for
screwing this up for me,

00:09:11.041 --> 00:09:16.005
I was doing the exact same thing
to my own children.

00:09:16.006 --> 00:09:18.510
Fin, believe me,
there are lots and

00:09:18.511 --> 00:09:20.780
lots of stories that
I'm not allowed to tell,

00:09:20.781 --> 00:09:23.840
but the authorized ones
I am allowed to tell,

00:09:23.841 --> 00:09:25.900
and one of the authorized
stories is about

00:09:25.901 --> 00:09:28.830
the day I found out
that when he was nine,

00:09:28.831 --> 00:09:32.630
that Fin didn't know how
to tie his own shoes.

00:09:32.631 --> 00:09:36.680
I found out because he hadn't
been attending PE class

00:09:36.681 --> 00:09:41.385
because he had been wearing
his brothers boots to school,

00:09:41.386 --> 00:09:43.705
which wouldn't be that big
of a deal except for the fact

00:09:43.706 --> 00:09:46.205
that his brother is
five years older than him,

00:09:46.206 --> 00:09:47.910
so they weren't
really boots so much,

00:09:47.911 --> 00:09:50.525
as they were like leg casts
because they went over,

00:09:50.526 --> 00:09:52.330
and he couldn't bend his knee.

00:09:52.331 --> 00:09:56.210
And so he couldn't do
PE and so he came on

00:09:56.211 --> 00:09:58.400
the same day actually
that I got that essay from

00:09:58.401 --> 00:09:59.650
the student about the fact that

00:09:59.651 --> 00:10:01.380
she no longer cared
about learning.

00:10:01.381 --> 00:10:02.840
I was trying to
get to the bottom

00:10:02.841 --> 00:10:04.270
of this sneaker situation,

00:10:04.271 --> 00:10:05.670
where are the
sneakers, why aren't

00:10:05.671 --> 00:10:07.560
you wearing the
sneakers to school,

00:10:07.561 --> 00:10:09.105
and I came to find out that

00:10:09.106 --> 00:10:10.700
the only sneakers he had access

00:10:10.701 --> 00:10:13.695
to were his sneakers with laces,

00:10:13.696 --> 00:10:15.840
and he wouldn't wear
those because he

00:10:15.841 --> 00:10:17.000
didn't know how to
tie them and he had

00:10:17.001 --> 00:10:18.245
been too ashamed to tell me,

00:10:18.246 --> 00:10:20.260
and he'd been too ashamed
to tell anyone else,

00:10:20.261 --> 00:10:22.990
and so I had to come to terms

00:10:22.991 --> 00:10:25.670
with the fact that
I had done that.

00:10:25.671 --> 00:10:26.730
Your kid is little,

00:10:26.731 --> 00:10:30.440
and they first get
manual dexterity and tying

00:10:30.441 --> 00:10:32.680
shoes is hard so

00:10:32.681 --> 00:10:34.660
you sit down you think
"This is going to be great."

00:10:34.661 --> 00:10:36.060
Totally Instagram worthy right?

00:10:36.061 --> 00:10:36.750
You're going to work on

00:10:36.751 --> 00:10:38.290
the shoes and he's
going to learn how to

00:10:38.291 --> 00:10:41.065
tie his shoes and everyone's
going to be so proud,

00:10:41.066 --> 00:10:42.920
except it's really stressful,

00:10:42.921 --> 00:10:44.380
and it's really hard,

00:10:44.381 --> 00:10:47.195
some kids get manual dexterity
later than others,

00:10:47.196 --> 00:10:51.645
and getting the laces just
right is really really hard.

00:10:51.646 --> 00:10:55.190
And so that first day I
didn't want to see him

00:10:55.191 --> 00:11:00.002
frustrated so I did it for
him. But just that one time.

00:11:00.003 --> 00:11:01.935
Except the next day we were in

00:11:01.936 --> 00:11:04.445
a rush and it's faster if

00:11:04.446 --> 00:11:09.005
I do it and so I did it that
day but just that one time.

00:11:09.006 --> 00:11:10.925
And now, I'm in a situation when

00:11:10.926 --> 00:11:12.665
I first started looking
at writing this book,

00:11:12.666 --> 00:11:15.535
he's nine and he's so humiliated
about the fact that he

00:11:15.536 --> 00:11:17.065
can't tie his own shoes
that he's just

00:11:17.066 --> 00:11:19.140
avoiding it by wearing
boots all the time.

00:11:19.141 --> 00:11:21.065
So that's where I was.
As pissed off as I

00:11:21.066 --> 00:11:23.205
wanted to be at you people,

00:11:23.206 --> 00:11:26.685
I had to realize
I am you people.

00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:29.365
So, off the high horse,

00:11:29.366 --> 00:11:31.185
I came and I started

00:11:31.186 --> 00:11:34.605
researching how these
extrinsic motivators,

00:11:34.606 --> 00:11:37.325
motivators that come from
outside of us like grades and

00:11:37.326 --> 00:11:38.855
points and scores and money

00:11:38.856 --> 00:11:40.750
and rewards and
all that kind of stuff,

00:11:40.751 --> 00:11:43.395
how that undermines
motivation and how

00:11:43.396 --> 00:11:46.130
that undermines
their ability to learn.

00:11:46.131 --> 00:11:49.175
And luckily, I'm also
an education journalist and

00:11:49.176 --> 00:11:52.375
so I was writing for the
Atlantic and the New York Times,

00:11:52.376 --> 00:11:54.985
I published an article
at the Atlantic

00:11:54.986 --> 00:11:58.545
on a research study
that was looking at how

00:11:58.546 --> 00:12:00.260
over parenting kids affects

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:02.685
their trajectory in school

00:12:02.686 --> 00:12:05.655
and that sort of started
this whole deep dive for me.

00:12:05.656 --> 00:12:07.900
Okay, so let's start
at the beginning.

00:12:07.901 --> 00:12:11.505
Dan Pink, TedTalk, Drive based

00:12:11.506 --> 00:12:14.805
a lot of his work on
another book by Edward Deci.

00:12:14.806 --> 00:12:16.285
And by the way, if you go

00:12:16.286 --> 00:12:19.105
to jessicalahey.com
under speaking,

00:12:19.106 --> 00:12:20.235
there's a big button that says

00:12:20.236 --> 00:12:22.840
download speaking
bibliography and

00:12:22.841 --> 00:12:25.090
it has every book
I'm going to mention,

00:12:25.091 --> 00:12:27.420
every YouTube video, every

00:12:27.421 --> 00:12:30.605
resource that I might even
touch on is all there.

00:12:30.606 --> 00:12:34.045
So just jessicalahe.com
big button downloads speaking

00:12:34.046 --> 00:12:37.940
bibliography under
speaking. Okay, Dan Pink.

00:12:37.941 --> 00:12:40.035
So Dan Pink based
a lot of his work,

00:12:40.036 --> 00:12:42.325
the early work on Edward Deci,

00:12:42.326 --> 00:12:43.515
Why We do What we do,

00:12:43.516 --> 00:12:45.250
The Science of Self-motivation.

00:12:45.251 --> 00:12:48.785
Edward Deci found along

00:12:48.786 --> 00:12:51.960
the way what we have come
to realize is there's

00:12:51.961 --> 00:12:56.660
40 years of
really great research

00:12:56.661 --> 00:12:59.175
on the effects of
extrinsic motivators.

00:12:59.176 --> 00:13:01.255
Not only 40 years

00:13:01.256 --> 00:13:03.325
of really good research,
there's metadata,

00:13:03.326 --> 00:13:05.525
there's studies of
that 40 years of

00:13:05.526 --> 00:13:08.055
research to show
one really clear thing,

00:13:08.056 --> 00:13:10.605
that when we lure people
when we give people

00:13:10.606 --> 00:13:14.450
extrinsic motivators in
order to get them to learn,

00:13:14.451 --> 00:13:17.780
we undermine their
interest in learning.

00:13:17.781 --> 00:13:19.935
From my perspective
as a teacher,

00:13:19.936 --> 00:13:21.985
it means if you
want your child to

00:13:21.986 --> 00:13:25.065
not want to learn math over

00:13:25.066 --> 00:13:27.390
the long-term
the fastest best way to

00:13:27.391 --> 00:13:28.865
sort of kill that off is

00:13:28.866 --> 00:13:31.195
to pay them for
their math grades.

00:13:31.196 --> 00:13:33.555
What's really interesting
about this research

00:13:33.556 --> 00:13:35.945
though is there's
also research showing

00:13:35.946 --> 00:13:37.510
that when we offer

00:13:37.511 --> 00:13:41.100
extrinsic motivators to
get kids to do stuff,

00:13:41.101 --> 00:13:44.340
we also mess with
their creativity.

00:13:44.341 --> 00:13:46.465
So, that's a problem

00:13:46.466 --> 00:13:48.575
because as a teacher,
from my perspective,

00:13:48.576 --> 00:13:52.035
education should be
a long-term creative endeavor

00:13:52.036 --> 00:13:53.590
and if we're screwing
around with it,

00:13:53.591 --> 00:13:55.325
from my perspective as with

00:13:55.326 --> 00:13:56.945
grades I mean those
are the tools I

00:13:56.946 --> 00:13:59.135
have and then from
the home perspective with

00:13:59.136 --> 00:14:00.145
money for grades and that

00:14:00.146 --> 00:14:01.695
kind of thing, we're
in big trouble.

00:14:01.696 --> 00:14:04.255
The creativity stuff is
really interesting when you

00:14:04.256 --> 00:14:09.425
try to control an artist's
output or offer them rewards,

00:14:09.426 --> 00:14:10.855
offer them trophies or

00:14:10.856 --> 00:14:12.945
money or whatever
for their output,

00:14:12.946 --> 00:14:14.485
it turns out that you

00:14:14.486 --> 00:14:17.175
can create a situation
where the artist or

00:14:17.176 --> 00:14:19.530
the child in
kindergarten who's being

00:14:19.531 --> 00:14:22.255
offered rewards for
painting in kindergarten,

00:14:22.256 --> 00:14:24.590
whether it's adult or
child it doesn't matter,

00:14:24.591 --> 00:14:26.380
they will spend less time on it,

00:14:26.381 --> 00:14:27.925
they will be less personally

00:14:27.926 --> 00:14:33.075
invested and the work itself
will be less creative.

00:14:33.076 --> 00:14:35.205
It turns out there are
these creativity experts,

00:14:35.206 --> 00:14:38.220
they can look at
this and assess this.

00:14:38.221 --> 00:14:39.845
The other problem is

00:14:39.846 --> 00:14:42.375
this and I'm going
to point directly at

00:14:42.376 --> 00:14:44.505
sticker charts mainly because
one of the questions I

00:14:44.506 --> 00:14:47.030
got originally before
I even came here today,

00:14:47.031 --> 00:14:49.330
thank you Anthony for
passing those along,

00:14:49.331 --> 00:14:51.330
was about sticker charts.

00:14:51.331 --> 00:14:53.990
One of the problems with
sticker charts is this,

00:14:53.991 --> 00:14:59.900
often we use sticker charts
in schools to shape behavior.

00:14:59.901 --> 00:15:03.135
There are a bunch of programs
out there that do this,

00:15:03.136 --> 00:15:06.510
there's class Dojo, there's
sticker charts at home,

00:15:06.511 --> 00:15:08.065
were you good at school today?

00:15:08.066 --> 00:15:09.935
If so, you get a big sticker.

00:15:09.936 --> 00:15:13.385
Schools of character, schools
that are really really

00:15:13.386 --> 00:15:15.105
deeply concerned with
character education

00:15:15.106 --> 00:15:16.550
don't use sticker charts.

00:15:16.551 --> 00:15:20.635
Because what sticker charts
teach children to do is to be

00:15:20.636 --> 00:15:25.175
good only when people
are watching, right?

00:15:25.176 --> 00:15:27.395
Because what is
the benefit of doing

00:15:27.396 --> 00:15:28.615
the right thing
unless someone is

00:15:28.616 --> 00:15:30.800
there to give you the sticker.

00:15:30.801 --> 00:15:33.240
If we're teaching children to

00:15:33.241 --> 00:15:35.735
only be good when
someone is watching,

00:15:35.736 --> 00:15:37.435
that's a problem
because schools of

00:15:37.436 --> 00:15:39.155
character one of
the things that they

00:15:39.156 --> 00:15:43.310
do is they talk about making
sure kids know the right,

00:15:43.311 --> 00:15:47.335
making sure kids do
the right and doing

00:15:47.336 --> 00:15:48.975
the right not just with people

00:15:48.976 --> 00:15:51.695
watching but when it's
just the right thing to do.

00:15:51.696 --> 00:15:54.015
When I talk about this stuff,

00:15:54.016 --> 00:15:59.415
I'm often reminded of admissions
counselor at Dartmouth.

00:15:59.416 --> 00:16:00.875
She's no longer there
but when she was

00:16:00.876 --> 00:16:02.405
there she wrote
this lovely op-ed for

00:16:02.406 --> 00:16:04.450
The New York Times about

00:16:04.451 --> 00:16:07.005
who they admitted to
Dartmouth and why.

00:16:07.006 --> 00:16:08.555
And she talked very
specifically about

00:16:08.556 --> 00:16:10.445
this one kid that they
decided to admit to

00:16:10.446 --> 00:16:12.380
Dartmouth because he had

00:16:12.381 --> 00:16:15.865
a letter of recommendation
from the janitor,

00:16:15.866 --> 00:16:18.180
the one of the grounds
keepers at his high school,

00:16:18.181 --> 00:16:19.355
and the groundskeeper said,

00:16:19.356 --> 00:16:21.030
"This is the kind
of kid that picks up

00:16:21.031 --> 00:16:23.655
trash on campus when
no one is watching."

00:16:23.656 --> 00:16:25.330
And this woman at
Dartmouth said,

00:16:25.331 --> 00:16:27.650
"That's what we want
for our community."

00:16:27.651 --> 00:16:31.225
So, that sort of stuff
is really important.

00:16:31.226 --> 00:16:33.925
Now, when I talk about
extrinsic motivators,

00:16:33.926 --> 00:16:36.595
I want to clarify
there's different kinds.

00:16:36.596 --> 00:16:39.265
There's the kinds that
are perceived as positive

00:16:39.266 --> 00:16:43.570
like money for grades
or stuff for grades.

00:16:43.571 --> 00:16:46.370
Usually when I go
around to schools,

00:16:46.371 --> 00:16:49.025
I talk to the kids during
the day and then I do

00:16:49.026 --> 00:16:50.705
professional development
with the teachers in

00:16:50.706 --> 00:16:51.825
the afternoon and then I

00:16:51.826 --> 00:16:53.475
talk to the parents
in the evening.

00:16:53.476 --> 00:16:55.375
And I do this little thing
with the kids,

00:16:55.376 --> 00:16:56.845
what I say to them is,

00:16:56.846 --> 00:16:59.590
"All teachers and
staff close your eyes.

00:16:59.591 --> 00:17:05.385
Now, raise your hand kids
if you get paid for grades."

00:17:05.386 --> 00:17:08.380
Depending on the
school district around

00:17:08.381 --> 00:17:10.965
15 percent or so

00:17:10.966 --> 00:17:13.530
between 15 and 20 percent
raised their hands.

00:17:13.531 --> 00:17:15.045
Then I said, "Put
your hands down.

00:17:15.046 --> 00:17:17.775
Now raise your hands
if you get anything in

00:17:17.776 --> 00:17:20.745
return for grade stuff
in return for grades."

00:17:20.746 --> 00:17:22.665
And it goes up a little
bit maybe like 20,

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:24.275
25 percent of the hands go up,

00:17:24.276 --> 00:17:25.990
depending on
the school district.

00:17:25.991 --> 00:17:27.735
And then I have them
put their hands down and

00:17:27.736 --> 00:17:29.625
I make sure the teachers
and the staff

00:17:29.626 --> 00:17:31.085
all have their eyes closed

00:17:31.086 --> 00:17:33.385
make them promise
and then I say,

00:17:33.386 --> 00:17:35.625
"Think about this really
carefully before you do

00:17:35.626 --> 00:17:38.625
it but raise your hands
if you really

00:17:38.626 --> 00:17:41.615
truly believe that
your parents love you

00:17:41.616 --> 00:17:43.965
more when you perform

00:17:43.966 --> 00:17:47.320
well in school versus
when you don't."

00:17:47.321 --> 00:17:50.175
In middle school, among
middle school kids,

00:17:50.176 --> 00:17:51.945
about 80 percent of the hands go

00:17:51.946 --> 00:17:54.235
up and among high school kids

00:17:54.236 --> 00:17:56.965
about 90 percent
of the hands go up.

00:17:57.175 --> 00:18:01.185
So extrinsic motivators
can be love.

00:18:01.186 --> 00:18:04.025
They can be us
loving our kids more

00:18:04.026 --> 00:18:06.745
showing more love
when they're giving us

00:18:06.746 --> 00:18:08.695
those grades that we
want from them and

00:18:08.696 --> 00:18:11.225
no I'm not an idiot
I know you can't

00:18:11.226 --> 00:18:14.285
we just react emotionally
differently when

00:18:14.286 --> 00:18:15.825
kids are doing
poorly versus when

00:18:15.826 --> 00:18:17.455
kids are doing well
because we're worried.

00:18:17.456 --> 00:18:18.975
We want our kids to do well in

00:18:18.976 --> 00:18:21.235
school but what I'm
talking about here

00:18:21.236 --> 00:18:22.665
more is giving them

00:18:22.666 --> 00:18:24.405
the idea that we
really and truly love

00:18:24.406 --> 00:18:25.875
them more when they give us

00:18:25.876 --> 00:18:28.135
those letters we want from them.

00:18:28.136 --> 00:18:31.685
There can also be negative
extrinsic motivators and

00:18:31.686 --> 00:18:32.695
when I'm talking about

00:18:32.696 --> 00:18:35.470
perception and those
can be things like,

00:18:35.471 --> 00:18:37.745
"You are grounded if you have

00:18:37.746 --> 00:18:40.735
anything less than a B
minus average," or,

00:18:40.736 --> 00:18:43.455
"We are going to take away

00:18:43.456 --> 00:18:46.545
your devices if you have
less than a whatever."

00:18:46.546 --> 00:18:49.525
And then there are
a couple that you

00:18:49.526 --> 00:18:53.175
wouldn't think of as extrinsic
motivators but they are.

00:18:53.176 --> 00:18:55.905
Let's say for example,

00:18:55.906 --> 00:18:59.590
if someone could
log onto a computer

00:18:59.591 --> 00:19:01.215
anytime they want and see

00:19:01.216 --> 00:19:04.065
their children's grades like 10,

00:19:04.066 --> 00:19:06.975
20, 30, 50 times a
day heck just keep

00:19:06.976 --> 00:19:10.410
it up on the desktop and just
hit refresh all day long.

00:19:10.411 --> 00:19:12.875
And it sounds like
I'm making a joke

00:19:12.876 --> 00:19:15.635
here and sort of using hyperbole

00:19:15.636 --> 00:19:17.650
but I'm not because from

00:19:17.651 --> 00:19:20.780
our perspective as school
administrators and teachers,

00:19:20.781 --> 00:19:23.070
we see how many times you login.

00:19:23.071 --> 00:19:27.297
We know and we talk about you.

00:19:27.298 --> 00:19:30.290
Mainly we talk about you because

00:19:30.291 --> 00:19:32.985
we are concerned as
teachers and administrators,

00:19:32.986 --> 00:19:35.070
that when you're
logging on constantly on

00:19:35.071 --> 00:19:38.300
the computer that
you are not having,

00:19:38.301 --> 00:19:41.140
channels of communication with

00:19:41.141 --> 00:19:42.840
your child may not be ideal.

00:19:42.841 --> 00:19:44.885
Because it looks
like the parents,

00:19:44.886 --> 00:19:46.430
from my perspective anyway,

00:19:46.431 --> 00:19:47.960
the parents that log
on the most tend to

00:19:47.961 --> 00:19:49.650
be the ones who don't really

00:19:49.651 --> 00:19:51.310
have really good open channels

00:19:51.311 --> 00:19:53.170
of communication
with their children.

00:19:53.171 --> 00:19:54.730
Now, there's another kind of

00:19:54.731 --> 00:19:57.040
extrinsic motivator that you
also may not think about,

00:19:57.041 --> 00:19:59.755
and that's tracking
your kids on their phones.

00:19:59.756 --> 00:20:01.230
And that's called surveillance.

00:20:01.231 --> 00:20:02.645
As is using the portal.

00:20:02.646 --> 00:20:05.700
And I'm not saying we can't
use any of these things,

00:20:05.701 --> 00:20:07.010
I'm just saying
that we have to be

00:20:07.011 --> 00:20:08.395
aware of them because if

00:20:08.396 --> 00:20:12.860
their effect is to undermine
motivation, and learning,

00:20:12.861 --> 00:20:14.320
as I'll get to in a second,

00:20:14.321 --> 00:20:16.890
then we're doing
our kids a disservice,

00:20:16.891 --> 00:20:18.210
if we're relying on

00:20:18.211 --> 00:20:20.840
those things in order
to shape their behavior.

00:20:20.841 --> 00:20:22.760
Again with extrinsic motivators,

00:20:22.761 --> 00:20:24.770
one of the things we have
to keep in mind is in

00:20:24.771 --> 00:20:28.340
the short-term they work
great, they are fantastic.

00:20:28.341 --> 00:20:29.980
If you want to boost motivation,

00:20:29.981 --> 00:20:31.490
if you want to sort of

00:20:31.491 --> 00:20:33.645
get the kid off
the starting block,

00:20:33.646 --> 00:20:36.350
then extrinsic motivators
can work great.

00:20:36.351 --> 00:20:37.660
If you want to offer your kid,

00:20:37.661 --> 00:20:39.580
you know a nice
sparkly Paris soccer

00:20:39.581 --> 00:20:41.900
shorts for going to
that first soccer practice,

00:20:41.901 --> 00:20:43.450
then great do that.

00:20:43.451 --> 00:20:47.020
But you can't have that be
your strategy moving forward

00:20:47.021 --> 00:20:48.790
because it will undermine

00:20:48.791 --> 00:20:51.050
their motivation and
their investment over time.

00:20:51.051 --> 00:20:53.340
So, here's what we do want.

00:20:53.341 --> 00:20:56.420
We want intrinsic motivation.

00:20:56.421 --> 00:20:58.610
Again I am not an idiot.

00:20:58.611 --> 00:21:00.050
I don't think that if you

00:21:00.051 --> 00:21:02.010
just do the things
I'm going to tell you

00:21:02.011 --> 00:21:03.600
about in a second
that your kid will

00:21:03.601 --> 00:21:05.910
be like 'quadratic formula,

00:21:05.911 --> 00:21:08.070
I got it. I'm so excited'.

00:21:08.071 --> 00:21:09.555
But you can up the chances

00:21:09.556 --> 00:21:11.600
that they will be
engaged for the sake of

00:21:11.601 --> 00:21:13.540
the learning itself
instead of for

00:21:13.541 --> 00:21:16.075
the sake of those numbers
and points and grades.

00:21:16.076 --> 00:21:18.640
Okay. If we're really lucky,

00:21:18.641 --> 00:21:20.100
the highest form of

00:21:20.101 --> 00:21:21.580
intrinsic motivation is this

00:21:21.581 --> 00:21:22.960
little something called flow.

00:21:22.961 --> 00:21:25.425
Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi
talks about flow.

00:21:25.426 --> 00:21:27.200
It's that state where you're

00:21:27.201 --> 00:21:28.980
doing something
that's difficult.

00:21:28.981 --> 00:21:30.890
It's not too easy,
it's not too hard,

00:21:30.891 --> 00:21:32.235
but it's difficult enough to

00:21:32.236 --> 00:21:34.570
really engage
your body or your mind.

00:21:34.571 --> 00:21:36.985
For me it's
cross-country skiing.

00:21:36.986 --> 00:21:39.290
It's hard for me, it's
a difficult thing,

00:21:39.291 --> 00:21:42.175
and I'm not doing it because
I'm going to win a race.

00:21:42.176 --> 00:21:47.035
I'm never I'm going to win
a race, but it's challenging.

00:21:47.036 --> 00:21:49.100
It's challenging to
my mind and to my body,

00:21:49.101 --> 00:21:51.020
and so time and
space falls away.

00:21:51.021 --> 00:21:52.510
For some people it's music.

00:21:52.511 --> 00:21:54.670
You know that's sort of
the classic flow state

00:21:54.671 --> 00:21:56.500
is often described as like jazz,

00:21:56.501 --> 00:21:57.740
where you're so engaged in it

00:21:57.741 --> 00:21:59.420
at the time and
space falls away.

00:21:59.421 --> 00:22:01.515
You don't get flow

00:22:01.516 --> 00:22:04.860
when you're being
extrinsically motivated,

00:22:04.861 --> 00:22:08.540
forced to do something for
the sake of something else.

00:22:08.541 --> 00:22:11.805
And if you think about extrinsic
motivators for a second,

00:22:11.806 --> 00:22:14.250
when I was young we used to have

00:22:14.251 --> 00:22:15.260
this thing where if we read

00:22:15.261 --> 00:22:16.770
a certain amount of
the summer pizza,

00:22:16.771 --> 00:22:19.765
Hut would give us
coupons for pizza.

00:22:19.766 --> 00:22:22.145
Think about that
for just a second.

00:22:22.146 --> 00:22:25.460
What's the most valuable thing
in that equation?

00:22:25.461 --> 00:22:27.680
Books or the pizza?

00:22:27.681 --> 00:22:28.990
Well the pizza is the reward.

00:22:28.991 --> 00:22:30.810
I mean if we really wanted to

00:22:30.811 --> 00:22:32.730
make books be
the valuable thing we

00:22:32.731 --> 00:22:37.305
would give kids books for
eating a lot of pizza, right?

00:22:37.306 --> 00:22:40.110
Okay. Intrinsic motivation.

00:22:40.111 --> 00:22:42.540
Edward DCci talks about
intrinsic motivation

00:22:42.541 --> 00:22:44.140
in his book and why
we do what we do,

00:22:44.141 --> 00:22:45.565
the science of self-motivation.

00:22:45.566 --> 00:22:49.725
It requires three
things. Number one.

00:22:49.726 --> 00:22:52.310
It requires autonomy,

00:22:52.311 --> 00:22:53.810
which is kind of like

00:22:53.811 --> 00:22:55.080
independence but a
little different.

00:22:55.081 --> 00:22:57.380
It has to do with
control over the details.

00:22:57.381 --> 00:22:59.590
Well, for those of you
in the room with toddlers

00:22:59.591 --> 00:23:02.420
you know full well
you do not say,

00:23:02.421 --> 00:23:04.750
"It's cold, would you like to

00:23:04.751 --> 00:23:06.690
wear a hat today?"
No, no, no, no.

00:23:06.691 --> 00:23:08.465
You say, "It's cold,

00:23:08.466 --> 00:23:11.350
do you want to wear
the red hat or the blue hat?"

00:23:11.351 --> 00:23:13.600
Right? Because then you're
getting some buy-in,

00:23:13.601 --> 00:23:14.800
because they get
to have a little

00:23:14.801 --> 00:23:16.610
autonomy over that decision.

00:23:16.611 --> 00:23:20.365
In the town where we live,
we're not big enough,

00:23:20.366 --> 00:23:22.655
I live in a very small town
in New Hampshire,

00:23:22.656 --> 00:23:25.090
we're not big enough to
have our own high school.

00:23:25.091 --> 00:23:26.690
So kids have school choice.

00:23:26.691 --> 00:23:28.860
And parents just why and

00:23:28.861 --> 00:23:31.795
worry and worry over
the choice because it's,

00:23:31.796 --> 00:23:34.830
you know it's such an
important decision, and it is.

00:23:34.831 --> 00:23:37.910
But I always come
back with, well,

00:23:37.911 --> 00:23:39.350
but if you let your kid

00:23:39.351 --> 00:23:42.630
choose then you're automatically
getting more buy-in,

00:23:42.631 --> 00:23:44.330
especially since
the high schools we have to

00:23:44.331 --> 00:23:46.530
choose from are all
pretty darn good.

00:23:46.531 --> 00:23:48.830
And if you get buy-in
from your kid you're

00:23:48.831 --> 00:23:51.000
more likely to have buy-in,
and that's autonomy.

00:23:51.001 --> 00:23:52.335
Control over the details.

00:23:52.336 --> 00:23:56.205
So autonomy number
one, number two,

00:23:56.206 --> 00:24:01.540
competence which I'm
so sorry to tell you

00:24:01.541 --> 00:24:06.520
parents is not the same
thing as confidence.

00:24:06.521 --> 00:24:09.490
Confidence we are really
good at as parents,

00:24:09.491 --> 00:24:11.160
we're not so good at instilling

00:24:11.161 --> 00:24:13.825
competence and I'll come
back to that in a minute.

00:24:13.826 --> 00:24:16.840
And finally, connection.

00:24:16.841 --> 00:24:18.440
Connection when I talk to

00:24:18.441 --> 00:24:20.890
teachers doing professional
development is this I

00:24:20.891 --> 00:24:22.700
could spend 90 minutes
just talking about

00:24:22.701 --> 00:24:24.935
connection because it
has to do with relevance,

00:24:24.936 --> 00:24:27.015
and this thing
called self-efficacy,

00:24:27.016 --> 00:24:28.450
and getting rid of

00:24:28.451 --> 00:24:29.685
learned helplessness and

00:24:29.686 --> 00:24:31.555
all this stuff
that's really fun.

00:24:31.556 --> 00:24:33.710
If your kids have teachers that

00:24:33.711 --> 00:24:37.445
make a subject come alive in
a way that makes them think,

00:24:37.446 --> 00:24:39.470
"I could go out into the world

00:24:39.471 --> 00:24:42.215
and do stuff with
this information",

00:24:42.216 --> 00:24:44.745
then please start sending
thank-you notes because

00:24:44.746 --> 00:24:47.975
that's really where the secret
source is with education.

00:24:47.976 --> 00:24:50.130
For parents it's
really pretty simple,

00:24:50.131 --> 00:24:51.915
and I'll come back
to that at the end.

00:24:51.916 --> 00:24:54.085
Alright, autonomy.

00:24:54.086 --> 00:24:57.140
In the book I talk about
the work of Wendy Grownek.

00:24:57.141 --> 00:25:00.490
She does research
on the way how we

00:25:00.491 --> 00:25:04.485
parent affects
kids motivation in learning.

00:25:04.486 --> 00:25:08.020
And one of the cool experiments
she did was this.

00:25:08.021 --> 00:25:10.440
She had mother infant pairs,

00:25:10.441 --> 00:25:11.875
and I'm sorry dads it just

00:25:11.876 --> 00:25:13.905
happened to be mothers
in this experiment.

00:25:13.906 --> 00:25:15.955
Mother infant pairs
came to her lab,

00:25:15.956 --> 00:25:17.780
and she designed a task for

00:25:17.781 --> 00:25:20.995
the kids that was
engaging and exciting,

00:25:20.996 --> 00:25:22.470
something they would want to do,

00:25:22.471 --> 00:25:24.855
but something that was a
little bit hard for them.

00:25:24.856 --> 00:25:27.370
She wanted it to be
challenging on purpose.

00:25:27.371 --> 00:25:29.050
Because she really
was interested

00:25:29.051 --> 00:25:30.345
not whether or
not the kid could,

00:25:30.346 --> 00:25:31.485
you know how they did the task,

00:25:31.486 --> 00:25:34.355
but how they reacted
when they got frustrated.

00:25:34.356 --> 00:25:37.100
And the instructions she
gave the parents were

00:25:37.101 --> 00:25:39.690
really quite generic on purpose.

00:25:39.691 --> 00:25:43.420
Be there with your child
while they complete this task.

00:25:43.421 --> 00:25:46.515
Because she wanted that to
be open to interpretation.

00:25:46.516 --> 00:25:48.175
Some of the parents were

00:25:48.176 --> 00:25:52.635
there while the child
completed the task.

00:25:52.636 --> 00:25:53.850
They let the child have

00:25:53.851 --> 00:25:56.085
control over how
the task was completed.

00:25:56.086 --> 00:25:57.715
If the kid got frustrated,

00:25:57.716 --> 00:25:59.700
they were there
to be supportive,

00:25:59.701 --> 00:26:02.610
they were what's called
autonomy supportive.

00:26:02.611 --> 00:26:05.265
They were there and supportive
of the child's autonomy,

00:26:05.266 --> 00:26:07.305
but they didn't take over.

00:26:07.306 --> 00:26:09.300
And those were referred to again

00:26:09.301 --> 00:26:11.125
as autonomy supportive parents.

00:26:11.126 --> 00:26:12.560
And then some of
the other parents,

00:26:12.561 --> 00:26:14.695
and I'm sure we don't
know any of these,

00:26:14.696 --> 00:26:16.545
were all over the task.

00:26:16.546 --> 00:26:18.930
They told the kid how to
do it, and where to do it,

00:26:18.931 --> 00:26:20.285
in what order to do the steps,

00:26:20.286 --> 00:26:22.300
and laid out the steps
very clearly.

00:26:22.301 --> 00:26:23.810
First do this, then do this.

00:26:23.811 --> 00:26:25.800
And by the way that behavior

00:26:25.801 --> 00:26:28.515
was amplified when she
told the parents that

00:26:28.516 --> 00:26:30.595
their child's work
would be evaluated by

00:26:30.596 --> 00:26:33.910
other parents after
the experiment.

00:26:33.911 --> 00:26:36.320
That went through
the roof. So these parents

00:26:36.321 --> 00:26:38.450
over here were called directive.

00:26:38.451 --> 00:26:40.850
Wendy also uses
the word controlling,

00:26:40.851 --> 00:26:42.140
but I'll stick with directive

00:26:42.141 --> 00:26:43.570
because I might
piss you off more

00:26:43.571 --> 00:26:45.010
later and so I'm
going to stick with

00:26:45.011 --> 00:26:47.145
the nice words now, directive.

00:26:47.146 --> 00:26:49.145
Autonomy, supportive, directive.

00:26:49.146 --> 00:26:50.360
Here's where it
gets interesting.

00:26:50.361 --> 00:26:53.140
Then she has the children
come back with the parents.

00:26:53.141 --> 00:26:58.175
This time she separates
the mothers from the children,

00:26:58.176 --> 00:27:00.775
and gives the children
another task that's engaging,

00:27:00.776 --> 00:27:02.400
exciting, something they want to

00:27:02.401 --> 00:27:04.550
do but again a little
bit frustrating.

00:27:04.551 --> 00:27:06.430
The children of the autonomy

00:27:06.431 --> 00:27:09.835
supportive parents got
excited about the task,

00:27:09.836 --> 00:27:11.170
and engaged in the task,

00:27:11.171 --> 00:27:12.745
and got started, and got going,

00:27:12.746 --> 00:27:14.570
and got frustrated,

00:27:14.571 --> 00:27:17.240
but were able to kind
of take a breath,

00:27:17.241 --> 00:27:18.670
look at it another way,

00:27:18.671 --> 00:27:20.500
think about the
instructions again,

00:27:20.501 --> 00:27:22.655
and sort of finish.

00:27:22.656 --> 00:27:25.120
Almost all of them
finished the task.

00:27:25.121 --> 00:27:29.080
The children of the directive
or controlling parents,

00:27:29.081 --> 00:27:32.635
they got into the task got
excited about the task,

00:27:32.636 --> 00:27:37.650
got frustrated, and almost
all of them cried and gave up.

00:27:37.651 --> 00:27:41.020
They were missing
whatever emotional wherewithal

00:27:41.021 --> 00:27:43.725
they needed in order to
sort of stick with it,

00:27:43.726 --> 00:27:45.885
and be okay with
being frustrated.

00:27:45.886 --> 00:27:48.440
Being okay with being
frustrated sort of is at

00:27:48.441 --> 00:27:51.480
the heart of
this whole equation.

00:27:51.481 --> 00:27:53.770
Because kids who
can't be frustrated,

00:27:53.771 --> 00:27:55.600
and we all know what
these kids look like, right?

00:27:55.601 --> 00:27:59.820
You take them to
their first gymnastics class

00:27:59.821 --> 00:28:00.860
and they can't do a cartwheel

00:28:00.861 --> 00:28:02.775
perfectly and they're
like, "Oh no, that's it.

00:28:02.776 --> 00:28:05.250
Forget it. I'll never
be able to do it ever."

00:28:05.251 --> 00:28:07.620
And that's really hard to get

00:28:07.621 --> 00:28:10.490
past and I'll talk

00:28:10.491 --> 00:28:12.605
a little bit about
that before we're done,

00:28:12.606 --> 00:28:15.515
and how to get over that.

00:28:15.516 --> 00:28:18.130
But when you can't be
frustrated there's a lot

00:28:18.131 --> 00:28:20.345
of learning that
can't happen too.

00:28:20.346 --> 00:28:23.580
And the way that these
directive parents are, you know,

00:28:23.581 --> 00:28:25.040
feeding the kid information and

00:28:25.041 --> 00:28:26.770
saying okay first do
this, second do this,

00:28:26.771 --> 00:28:29.340
and not wanting to see
our kids get frustrated,

00:28:29.341 --> 00:28:30.730
that is so natural.

00:28:30.731 --> 00:28:32.670
I hate seeing my kids
get frustrated.

00:28:32.671 --> 00:28:35.000
I hate seeing my students
get frustrated.

00:28:35.001 --> 00:28:36.460
I have the answers.

00:28:36.461 --> 00:28:38.800
It would be so great
if I just gave them

00:28:38.801 --> 00:28:40.030
the information and then

00:28:40.031 --> 00:28:42.155
no one has to be
frustrated ever,

00:28:42.156 --> 00:28:44.490
and frankly it makes me
look like a better teacher.

00:28:44.491 --> 00:28:46.910
Because now my students are
getting the right answer,

00:28:46.911 --> 00:28:48.640
whether or not they
really understand

00:28:48.641 --> 00:28:50.845
the information very well,

00:28:50.846 --> 00:28:53.340
that's a whole other question.

00:28:53.341 --> 00:28:54.990
Why do I care so much?

00:28:54.991 --> 00:28:56.840
Well, when kids are
really, really little,

00:28:56.841 --> 00:28:58.190
a kid who cries and gives up,

00:28:58.191 --> 00:28:59.940
and can't stick with something,

00:28:59.941 --> 00:29:01.575
that's frustrating as a parent,

00:29:01.576 --> 00:29:03.040
but eventually they're
going to end up in

00:29:03.041 --> 00:29:05.055
my classroom, or a classroom.

00:29:05.056 --> 00:29:06.910
And this is where
the rubber really

00:29:06.911 --> 00:29:09.195
hits the road because
what ends up happening is,

00:29:09.196 --> 00:29:14.760
kids who can't be frustrated
can't learn as well.

00:29:14.761 --> 00:29:20.000
I would always rather
have a perfectly average,

00:29:20.001 --> 00:29:22.310
and I use the word
perfectly on purpose,

00:29:22.311 --> 00:29:25.870
a perfectly average child
of average intelligence.

00:29:25.871 --> 00:29:27.700
I would much rather
have a kid that's of

00:29:27.701 --> 00:29:30.340
average intelligence but
who can be frustrated,

00:29:30.341 --> 00:29:33.780
than a kid who is brilliant
beyond measure but gives

00:29:33.781 --> 00:29:35.240
up the second they feel

00:29:35.241 --> 00:29:37.315
like they maybe can't
do something right.

00:29:37.316 --> 00:29:39.430
Because that kid who can be

00:29:39.431 --> 00:29:41.855
frustrated is more
teachable, and here's why.

00:29:41.856 --> 00:29:44.175
One of the most
powerful teaching tools

00:29:44.176 --> 00:29:45.685
I have as a teacher,

00:29:45.686 --> 00:29:47.940
there are two I talk
about frequently.

00:29:47.941 --> 00:29:50.735
One of them I talk about
mostly with teachers,

00:29:50.736 --> 00:29:52.400
but the one I talked
about with parents is

00:29:52.401 --> 00:29:55.565
this one called
desirable difficulties.

00:29:55.566 --> 00:29:57.535
There's a fantastic book that

00:29:57.536 --> 00:29:59.430
it's on the bibliography
that came out a couple of

00:29:59.431 --> 00:30:01.290
years ago called
Make It Stick from

00:30:01.291 --> 00:30:03.615
Harvard University Press,
three authors.

00:30:03.616 --> 00:30:06.770
They talk about desirable
difficulties in this book

00:30:06.771 --> 00:30:08.010
because it's so powerful.

00:30:08.011 --> 00:30:10.305
Here's what desirable
difficulties can do.

00:30:10.306 --> 00:30:13.640
If I give all of you
a paragraph about

00:30:13.641 --> 00:30:16.130
jellyfish and half of

00:30:16.131 --> 00:30:18.360
you have a paragraph
that's easy to read.

00:30:18.361 --> 00:30:19.770
The text is really clear,

00:30:19.771 --> 00:30:21.540
the words you don't have
to look them up it's

00:30:21.541 --> 00:30:23.585
all in language
you can understand.

00:30:23.586 --> 00:30:25.060
And half of you have a piece of

00:30:25.061 --> 00:30:26.775
paper that's difficult to read.

00:30:26.776 --> 00:30:28.580
Maybe some of
the letters are reversed,

00:30:28.581 --> 00:30:29.850
maybe some of the words are more

00:30:29.851 --> 00:30:31.385
difficult then you
have to look them up

00:30:31.386 --> 00:30:34.590
but essentially the information
is the same and I say in

00:30:34.591 --> 00:30:36.330
five minutes I'm
going to give you

00:30:36.331 --> 00:30:39.095
a quiz on this paragraph
on jellyfish.

00:30:39.096 --> 00:30:41.070
You people over
here who have the

00:30:41.071 --> 00:30:42.735
difficult to read
piece of paper,

00:30:42.736 --> 00:30:46.480
you are going to slay
them on the quiz in

00:30:46.481 --> 00:30:49.400
the short-term and
then in the long-term

00:30:49.401 --> 00:30:50.910
if you all come back and take

00:30:50.911 --> 00:30:52.780
this quiz again in two weeks,

00:30:52.781 --> 00:30:55.755
you're really going to
kill them on the quiz.

00:30:55.756 --> 00:30:58.210
Because what your brain
has done when you

00:30:58.211 --> 00:31:01.545
had to work a little bit
harder to get information in,

00:31:01.546 --> 00:31:04.165
your brain said, "Oh

00:31:04.166 --> 00:31:06.470
this is not short-term
memory stuff."

00:31:06.471 --> 00:31:07.700
Short-term memory stuff is

00:31:07.701 --> 00:31:09.330
like the number
for the pizza guy.

00:31:09.331 --> 00:31:11.130
Something that you can
keep in your head for a

00:31:11.131 --> 00:31:13.305
second but then it's
fine, it can go away.

00:31:13.306 --> 00:31:15.040
When you have to work
a little bit harder

00:31:15.041 --> 00:31:16.855
to get the information
in your head,

00:31:16.856 --> 00:31:18.545
your brain says, "Oh No No.

00:31:18.546 --> 00:31:20.005
This is long-term memory stuff."

00:31:20.006 --> 00:31:21.445
Clearly this is important.

00:31:21.446 --> 00:31:23.030
This is more
challenging it causes

00:31:23.031 --> 00:31:25.010
the brain automatically
to do this thing called

00:31:25.011 --> 00:31:26.580
encoding where it takes

00:31:26.581 --> 00:31:28.020
the information moves it from

00:31:28.021 --> 00:31:29.635
short-term to long-term memory.

00:31:29.636 --> 00:31:31.350
Short-term memory is not a place

00:31:31.351 --> 00:31:33.140
where we can manipulate
information very

00:31:33.141 --> 00:31:34.750
well and it's
definitely not a place

00:31:34.751 --> 00:31:36.675
where we store stuff long-term.

00:31:36.676 --> 00:31:40.240
So if we want kids to do
"oh I don't know" Know

00:31:40.241 --> 00:31:41.970
information well
enough to be able to

00:31:41.971 --> 00:31:44.380
apply it in a novel situation,

00:31:44.381 --> 00:31:45.870
it needs to be in long-term it

00:31:45.871 --> 00:31:47.925
can't just be sitting
in short-term and

00:31:47.926 --> 00:31:49.820
what you people did
over here to learn about

00:31:49.821 --> 00:31:52.050
the jellyfish was you crammed.

00:31:52.051 --> 00:31:54.330
You heard you had
five minutes to learn it all,

00:31:54.331 --> 00:31:56.090
and so you just shoved
it in your head.

00:31:56.091 --> 00:31:57.180
Maybe those of you who brought

00:31:57.181 --> 00:31:59.445
highlighters with
you, you highlighted.

00:31:59.446 --> 00:32:01.150
Please let's take highlighters

00:32:01.151 --> 00:32:03.080
away from kids because all they

00:32:03.081 --> 00:32:07.370
do is highlight everything
and they say, "That's okay.

00:32:07.371 --> 00:32:09.160
I'll memorize all of it."

00:32:09.161 --> 00:32:11.640
That's cramming. Those
of us who have done

00:32:11.641 --> 00:32:13.000
any amount of cramming in

00:32:13.001 --> 00:32:14.580
our lives know it does not work.

00:32:14.581 --> 00:32:16.695
It definitely doesn't
work over the short-term,

00:32:16.696 --> 00:32:20.050
it really doesn't work
over the long-term.

00:32:20.051 --> 00:32:23.980
Those of us who cram we try
to shove it all in there,

00:32:23.981 --> 00:32:26.260
stick a cork in
our ear and then walk

00:32:26.261 --> 00:32:28.850
this way to the task so it
doesn't all dribble out.

00:32:28.851 --> 00:32:31.535
Some fun information when
I get to talk to kids,

00:32:31.536 --> 00:32:32.710
I talk to them a lot about

00:32:32.711 --> 00:32:34.170
sleep and the
importance of sleep

00:32:34.171 --> 00:32:37.695
because I can't overestimate
the importance of sleep.

00:32:37.696 --> 00:32:40.090
One of the pieces of information
I give them is there are

00:32:40.091 --> 00:32:43.720
studies that have shown
that if you have one hour,

00:32:43.721 --> 00:32:45.430
you know the are kids
like, "Oh one more hour.

00:32:45.431 --> 00:32:46.900
I'll turn the lights
off in one more hour.

00:32:46.901 --> 00:32:48.640
Just want to do
one more hour of studying."

00:32:48.641 --> 00:32:51.305
And you're like oh my kid is
so diligent and fantastic.

00:32:51.306 --> 00:32:53.275
What you can tell your child is,

00:32:53.276 --> 00:32:54.700
one extra hour of

00:32:54.701 --> 00:32:57.370
sleep is more powerful

00:32:57.371 --> 00:32:59.425
because of the
memory consolidation,

00:32:59.426 --> 00:33:01.735
than one more hour of studying.

00:33:01.736 --> 00:33:03.920
So please when I tell kids

00:33:03.921 --> 00:33:05.010
how much sleep
they're supposed to be

00:33:05.011 --> 00:33:06.425
getting they just laugh me.

00:33:06.426 --> 00:33:08.585
Teenagers are supposed
to still be getting

00:33:08.586 --> 00:33:10.200
around nine and a
half hours of sleep

00:33:10.201 --> 00:33:11.780
at night and they're
getting nowhere near that.

00:33:11.781 --> 00:33:12.870
A lot of kids are getting

00:33:12.871 --> 00:33:14.545
somewhere closer
to six and a half.

00:33:14.546 --> 00:33:18.850
And in terms of
learning it's sleep.

00:33:18.851 --> 00:33:21.295
Sleep is one of the most
important things we can do.

00:33:21.296 --> 00:33:24.780
So, tell your kid if they
say "I want one more hour."

00:33:24.781 --> 00:33:27.330
Say "Look I learned from
this woman Miss Lei She's

00:33:27.331 --> 00:33:29.200
an education researcher
and she found out

00:33:29.201 --> 00:33:32.310
that one more hour of sleep
is more valuable than."

00:33:32.311 --> 00:33:35.080
And that's why the American
Academy of Pediatrics

00:33:35.081 --> 00:33:37.080
came together a couple
of years ago to issue

00:33:37.081 --> 00:33:38.530
a statement saying
that schools should

00:33:38.531 --> 00:33:40.500
open no earlier than 8.30

00:33:40.501 --> 00:33:42.040
in the morning because
an extra hour of

00:33:42.041 --> 00:33:44.455
sleep is such a powerful
thing for teenagers.

00:33:44.456 --> 00:33:46.760
Just on a little tangent.

00:33:46.761 --> 00:33:48.750
If your teenager
say late at night,

00:33:48.751 --> 00:33:50.165
"Am just not sleepy yet."

00:33:50.166 --> 00:33:52.090
They're not lying,
they really aren't.

00:33:52.091 --> 00:33:54.865
Teenagers have this thing
called sleep phase delay.

00:33:54.866 --> 00:33:57.190
Where their brain
squirts out melatonin

00:33:57.191 --> 00:33:59.520
and other chemicals
and makes them

00:33:59.521 --> 00:34:02.540
sleepy later but
the other problem is that one of

00:34:02.541 --> 00:34:05.010
the other things
that teenagers brains

00:34:05.011 --> 00:34:07.735
don't do very well is
perceive how tired they are.

00:34:07.736 --> 00:34:10.720
So they could be
exhausted and they're

00:34:10.721 --> 00:34:13.620
not as good as adults
are at knowing that.

00:34:13.621 --> 00:34:15.710
Which is why I have

00:34:15.711 --> 00:34:17.040
to tap on their shoulder when

00:34:17.041 --> 00:34:18.590
they're drooling on the desk.

00:34:18.591 --> 00:34:20.940
It's also why when schools do

00:34:20.941 --> 00:34:23.665
move their school start
times an hour later,

00:34:23.666 --> 00:34:27.910
not only does it improve
test scores, grades,

00:34:27.911 --> 00:34:30.260
behavior, it decreases
the number of

00:34:30.261 --> 00:34:33.575
car accidents
caused by teenagers.

00:34:33.576 --> 00:34:35.300
An hour extra of sleep is

00:34:35.301 --> 00:34:38.015
incredibly powerful thing.
Really really powerful.

00:34:38.016 --> 00:34:40.190
Okay. And by the way if you get

00:34:40.191 --> 00:34:42.080
the American Academy
of Pediatrics to come

00:34:42.081 --> 00:34:44.410
together and issue
a policy statement on something,

00:34:44.411 --> 00:34:47.840
it's a miracle and it really
means that they're serious.

00:34:47.841 --> 00:34:50.040
So when they come out
and say schools should

00:34:50.041 --> 00:34:52.590
start no earlier than
8.30 for high school,

00:34:52.591 --> 00:34:54.330
this is not for younger kids
because they don't have

00:34:54.331 --> 00:34:57.370
the sleep phase delay issue
but for high school.

00:34:57.371 --> 00:34:59.590
It's important stuff.

00:34:59.591 --> 00:35:00.775
It's really worth listening.

00:35:00.776 --> 00:35:02.950
And yes it's incredibly
difficult to move

00:35:02.951 --> 00:35:04.240
school start times because

00:35:04.241 --> 00:35:06.225
of sports and extracurriculars.

00:35:06.226 --> 00:35:08.040
It really seems to be worth it.

00:35:08.041 --> 00:35:09.345
These school districts
that have done it.

00:35:09.346 --> 00:35:12.350
Okay. Autonomy, still
overhearing competence.

00:35:12.351 --> 00:35:13.860
The difference
between competence and

00:35:13.861 --> 00:35:15.520
confidence is really
important to know.

00:35:15.521 --> 00:35:18.205
Confidence is
that empty feeling of,

00:35:18.206 --> 00:35:21.970
"Yeah my parents tell me
all the time how smart I am.

00:35:21.971 --> 00:35:24.390
It's going to be
fine. It'll be great."

00:35:24.391 --> 00:35:26.690
That kid who comes to
Kindergarten and says,

00:35:26.691 --> 00:35:28.630
"I'm here and I'm

00:35:28.631 --> 00:35:30.160
the best reader in the class

00:35:30.161 --> 00:35:32.110
because my parents told me so."

00:35:32.111 --> 00:35:34.130
That's great.

00:35:34.131 --> 00:35:35.730
Optimism is fantastic.

00:35:35.731 --> 00:35:37.240
I am an optimistic person,

00:35:37.241 --> 00:35:40.130
I think it's what keeps
me from just falling

00:35:40.131 --> 00:35:43.460
down with despair over the state
of education some days.

00:35:43.461 --> 00:35:47.000
But it needs to be paired
with experience and

00:35:47.001 --> 00:35:51.050
competence is confidence
paired with actual experience,

00:35:51.051 --> 00:35:53.615
doing something, screwing it up,

00:35:53.616 --> 00:35:55.880
figuring out what to
leave behind and what to

00:35:55.881 --> 00:35:58.765
bring forward with you and
trying and doing it again.

00:35:58.766 --> 00:36:02.030
I got to work on
this really fun television show

00:36:02.031 --> 00:36:04.880
for really young kids called
the Stinky and Dirty show,

00:36:04.881 --> 00:36:06.285
it runs on Amazon.

00:36:06.286 --> 00:36:09.290
And in that show we have
these two characters who

00:36:09.291 --> 00:36:12.430
have to get these tasks done and

00:36:12.431 --> 00:36:15.770
inevitably their first
sought of ideas about

00:36:15.771 --> 00:36:17.260
how they're going to
get these tasks done are

00:36:17.261 --> 00:36:19.050
classic like
preschooler tat like,

00:36:19.051 --> 00:36:20.470
"We'll wait for the moon to come

00:36:20.471 --> 00:36:21.880
up and then we'll
throw a last sue

00:36:21.881 --> 00:36:23.270
over the moon and it'll pull

00:36:23.271 --> 00:36:25.140
the bowling ball
up or whatever."

00:36:25.141 --> 00:36:27.310
And they come to find
that that's not going to

00:36:27.311 --> 00:36:29.290
work but they don't
say, "Well that's it.

00:36:29.291 --> 00:36:30.950
I'm too stupid to do this."

00:36:30.951 --> 00:36:32.150
They come up with that

00:36:32.151 --> 00:36:33.780
because they're supportive
of each other and

00:36:33.781 --> 00:36:36.620
because they are working
toward a common goal

00:36:36.621 --> 00:36:38.000
and because they're
resilient little

00:36:38.001 --> 00:36:39.670
creatures, little
characters there.

00:36:39.671 --> 00:36:41.815
A dump truck and a digger.

00:36:41.816 --> 00:36:45.595
They move forward with
the next iteration of the plan.

00:36:45.596 --> 00:36:46.980
That's competence.

00:36:46.981 --> 00:36:48.730
In the classroom
it looks like this,

00:36:48.731 --> 00:36:51.170
I give kids something
let's say we're

00:36:51.171 --> 00:36:53.815
in Latin class sorry if
I give anyone flashbacks.

00:36:53.816 --> 00:36:55.510
And I'm teaching the nominative

00:36:55.511 --> 00:36:57.420
and accusative case
and I say "Okay.

00:36:57.421 --> 00:36:59.395
Here's the sentence Puella,

00:36:59.396 --> 00:37:03.100
Puerum, Ahmad, the girl
loves the boys."

00:37:03.101 --> 00:37:06.100
Standard word order
in Latin and they

00:37:06.101 --> 00:37:07.630
get comfortable
with that and then

00:37:07.631 --> 00:37:09.365
I go over here and I say "Okay.

00:37:09.366 --> 00:37:11.035
This one's a little harder.

00:37:11.036 --> 00:37:13.500
Puerum, Puella, Ahmad."

00:37:13.501 --> 00:37:15.040
And they freak out at first.

00:37:15.041 --> 00:37:16.740
They're like "That
makes no sense.

00:37:16.741 --> 00:37:18.320
You're missing the wrong place."

00:37:18.321 --> 00:37:20.005
And then they calm
down and I say,

00:37:20.006 --> 00:37:22.500
"No you have
the skills to do this.

00:37:22.501 --> 00:37:24.155
Just be quiet for a second."

00:37:24.156 --> 00:37:26.050
In my classroom you're
not allowed to speak for

00:37:26.051 --> 00:37:27.990
a few seconds after I present

00:37:27.991 --> 00:37:30.250
a question because that favors

00:37:30.251 --> 00:37:31.590
the extroverts to throw

00:37:31.591 --> 00:37:33.110
their hands up in
the air and they like,

00:37:33.111 --> 00:37:34.655
and they don't know what
they're going to say.

00:37:34.656 --> 00:37:37.930
They have no idea. But
you got to say that let.

00:37:37.931 --> 00:37:39.780
I know because I'm one of those.

00:37:39.781 --> 00:37:42.750
In law school I figured as
my hand was up in the air,

00:37:42.751 --> 00:37:45.210
something would fall out
of my mouth that made

00:37:45.211 --> 00:37:47.625
sense and plus if I
volunteered enough,

00:37:47.626 --> 00:37:49.180
that would mean that
maybe I wouldn't get

00:37:49.181 --> 00:37:51.840
called on like randomly.

00:37:51.841 --> 00:37:53.912
It totally works.

00:37:53.913 --> 00:37:56.645
But introverted kids often

00:37:56.646 --> 00:37:59.025
in the classroom need
a few more moments to think.

00:37:59.026 --> 00:38:01.565
And so, that was a big discovery
I made as a teacher,

00:38:01.566 --> 00:38:03.545
is that if I stopped
calling on kids right away,

00:38:03.546 --> 00:38:06.865
I could actually level
the playing field a bit.

00:38:06.866 --> 00:38:10.205
So the problem
with these kids is,

00:38:10.206 --> 00:38:12.075
then they say, "Oh, I don't
know. That's so hard."

00:38:12.076 --> 00:38:14.250
But then, they realize,
okay, no, no, no.

00:38:14.251 --> 00:38:15.685
I did it this way over here.

00:38:15.686 --> 00:38:17.115
Mrs. Lahey has faith in me.

00:38:17.116 --> 00:38:19.115
She thinks I can do it
all. I see what you did.

00:38:19.116 --> 00:38:20.515
You just reversed the two words.

00:38:20.516 --> 00:38:22.145
I get it. And then,

00:38:22.146 --> 00:38:23.945
I give them something
even harder.

00:38:23.946 --> 00:38:26.285
And whether that's in
the form of homework,

00:38:26.286 --> 00:38:28.415
and I do not have time to talk

00:38:28.416 --> 00:38:30.520
about the way I
feel about homework,

00:38:30.521 --> 00:38:31.905
I've written about
it extensively at

00:38:31.906 --> 00:38:33.700
The New York Times
and The Atlantic.

00:38:33.701 --> 00:38:35.590
The research is pretty clear.

00:38:35.591 --> 00:38:37.465
I don't have time
to talk about that.

00:38:37.466 --> 00:38:39.135
But if I give them, let's say,

00:38:39.136 --> 00:38:42.495
homework that's built
on what we did in class,

00:38:42.496 --> 00:38:44.000
but is a little bit harder,

00:38:44.001 --> 00:38:45.275
incorporating, I don't know,

00:38:45.276 --> 00:38:47.920
some of those desirable
difficulties in,

00:38:47.921 --> 00:38:52.225
I need the kids to
be able to stay calm

00:38:52.226 --> 00:38:55.665
and to trust that I'm

00:38:55.666 --> 00:38:57.165
not just putting
them out there with

00:38:57.166 --> 00:38:59.045
this difficult stuff that
they can't possibly do.

00:38:59.046 --> 00:39:00.485
And it's the kids who can

00:39:00.486 --> 00:39:01.985
be frustrated who
can push through.

00:39:01.986 --> 00:39:06.025
It's the kids who feel
competent, not just confident.

00:39:06.026 --> 00:39:08.675
The confidence game really

00:39:08.676 --> 00:39:10.460
comes out of the
self-esteem movement.

00:39:10.461 --> 00:39:11.985
Total failure by the way.

00:39:11.986 --> 00:39:13.685
Self-esteem movement, what we

00:39:13.686 --> 00:39:15.995
hoped was that if
we told our kids

00:39:15.996 --> 00:39:17.965
enough times how fantastic and

00:39:17.966 --> 00:39:20.325
wonderful and gifted
and talented they are,

00:39:20.326 --> 00:39:22.325
that they would have this Star

00:39:22.326 --> 00:39:24.255
Wars force field
of wonderfulness

00:39:24.256 --> 00:39:26.165
around them so that when
they would go off to

00:39:26.166 --> 00:39:28.195
school and if someone
said something mean,

00:39:28.196 --> 00:39:31.015
it would like pew and
go off to the side.

00:39:31.016 --> 00:39:32.600
But they would
come home with like

00:39:32.601 --> 00:39:35.380
their force field of
wonderfulness intact.

00:39:35.381 --> 00:39:37.685
It doesn't work that way.

00:39:37.686 --> 00:39:41.495
The research on self-esteem
and praise shows,

00:39:41.496 --> 00:39:42.605
there's a study
that came out just

00:39:42.606 --> 00:39:43.765
recently, was
really interesting.

00:39:43.766 --> 00:39:46.905
It showed that for kids who
have really low self-esteem,

00:39:46.906 --> 00:39:48.845
the kids we really need to buoy

00:39:48.846 --> 00:39:51.700
up with our words of
support and praise,

00:39:51.701 --> 00:39:56.285
if we tell those kids that
they're so smart, gifted,

00:39:56.286 --> 00:40:00.675
talented, perfect, we don't
raise their self-esteem.

00:40:00.676 --> 00:40:02.325
We lower it.

00:40:02.326 --> 00:40:04.855
Because for kids like that,

00:40:04.856 --> 00:40:09.160
and I often remember myself
sitting there in Algebra one,

00:40:09.161 --> 00:40:11.720
thinking, ''Oh my gosh!

00:40:11.721 --> 00:40:13.275
I'm the only person who does

00:40:13.276 --> 00:40:15.550
not understand any of this.''

00:40:15.551 --> 00:40:17.925
And if I had gone home and

00:40:17.926 --> 00:40:20.400
my parents were telling me
constantly how gifted I was,

00:40:20.401 --> 00:40:23.020
my school reality and
home reality would not match.

00:40:23.021 --> 00:40:25.690
And that is really
disturbing to kids.

00:40:25.691 --> 00:40:27.225
When I speak to kids,

00:40:27.226 --> 00:40:28.625
one my favorite grades
to speak to is

00:40:28.626 --> 00:40:31.025
sixth grade because they
have the best the questions.

00:40:31.026 --> 00:40:33.020
And recently, I was at
a school in New York.

00:40:33.021 --> 00:40:34.545
And this one kid
raised his hand,

00:40:34.546 --> 00:40:36.975
and he said, ''Yeah.

00:40:36.976 --> 00:40:38.715
I don't know if
it's just me or not,

00:40:38.716 --> 00:40:42.345
but my parents tell me I am
perfect at everything I do,

00:40:42.346 --> 00:40:44.975
and I just don't think
that can be right.''

00:40:44.976 --> 00:40:48.315
And I said, ''It's
not just you sweetie.

00:40:48.316 --> 00:40:49.610
And as wonderful is,

00:40:49.611 --> 00:40:51.835
as I think I assume you are.

00:40:51.836 --> 00:40:54.635
It's probably true that you're
not perfect at everything,

00:40:54.636 --> 00:40:56.105
and that's good
because we shouldn't

00:40:56.106 --> 00:40:57.625
be perfect at everything
right off the bad.

00:40:57.626 --> 00:41:00.285
That means we're probably
not trying very hard.''

00:41:00.286 --> 00:41:03.735
So, telling kids how
wonderful and perfect

00:41:03.736 --> 00:41:06.595
they are all the time can
mess with their heads.

00:41:06.596 --> 00:41:08.510
Not only that,
those of you who are

00:41:08.511 --> 00:41:10.445
familiar at all with Carol Dweck

00:41:10.446 --> 00:41:11.985
and her work on mindsets,

00:41:11.986 --> 00:41:13.375
if you've only read articles

00:41:13.376 --> 00:41:15.355
about Carol Dweck and mindsets,

00:41:15.356 --> 00:41:17.740
I would encourage you
to read the whole book,

00:41:17.741 --> 00:41:20.945
because the way
journalism intends,

00:41:20.946 --> 00:41:23.155
I hope this doesn't come
as a shocker to anyone.

00:41:23.156 --> 00:41:24.680
But what often happens is,

00:41:24.681 --> 00:41:26.215
I go to my editor, and I say,

00:41:26.216 --> 00:41:28.525
''I have this article
I want to write on

00:41:28.526 --> 00:41:32.075
this amazing breakthrough
education theory,

00:41:32.076 --> 00:41:35.245
and I'm going to need
like 2,000 more words.''

00:41:35.246 --> 00:41:37.105
And she's like,
''Okay. Sounds great.

00:41:37.106 --> 00:41:39.660
You can write it, but
you get 500 words.''

00:41:39.661 --> 00:41:42.460
So we tend to oversimplify
just because that's how.

00:41:42.461 --> 00:41:44.715
They're all terribly
editors or terribly

00:41:44.716 --> 00:41:47.025
concerned that your attention
span is about this big.

00:41:47.026 --> 00:41:49.050
So we need to prove them wrong.

00:41:49.051 --> 00:41:51.485
So I go off and I
have to write about

00:41:51.486 --> 00:41:55.220
something in making a list,
which is ridiculous.

00:41:55.221 --> 00:41:57.105
Those of you who have only

00:41:57.106 --> 00:41:58.515
read about Carol
Dweck's research,

00:41:58.516 --> 00:42:00.115
what you'd probably think it

00:42:00.116 --> 00:42:02.680
means is that we
should only praise

00:42:02.681 --> 00:42:05.535
our children for their effort

00:42:05.536 --> 00:42:09.845
and never tell them that
they're smart, which is stupid.

00:42:09.846 --> 00:42:11.065
Because if I were to run at

00:42:11.066 --> 00:42:12.885
that door and I
don't know that I

00:42:12.886 --> 00:42:14.285
have to press that bar right

00:42:14.286 --> 00:42:16.025
there to make the door unlatch,

00:42:16.026 --> 00:42:18.330
but I just keep running at

00:42:18.331 --> 00:42:20.825
the door and bashing
my body against it,

00:42:20.826 --> 00:42:22.640
and you keep saying, ''Oh, Jess,

00:42:22.641 --> 00:42:24.460
good effort, keep going.''

00:42:24.461 --> 00:42:26.515
You are terrible teachers.

00:42:26.516 --> 00:42:28.120
I'm not learning very much.

00:42:28.121 --> 00:42:30.515
I'm not going to
trust you anymore.

00:42:30.516 --> 00:42:33.330
We're sort of, neither one
of us is getting anywhere.

00:42:33.331 --> 00:42:35.845
But if you're
supportive in a way

00:42:35.846 --> 00:42:36.985
that a lot of teachers know

00:42:36.986 --> 00:42:38.430
works pretty well
in the classroom is,

00:42:38.431 --> 00:42:39.875
''Oh, I see you're
having some problems.

00:42:39.876 --> 00:42:40.955
Stop for a second.

00:42:40.956 --> 00:42:42.180
Look at the door. Is there

00:42:42.181 --> 00:42:43.455
anything you're not thinking of?

00:42:43.456 --> 00:42:45.405
Is there anything you
haven't considered?"

00:42:45.406 --> 00:42:46.815
That's what great teaching and

00:42:46.816 --> 00:42:48.175
parenting looks
like because that

00:42:48.176 --> 00:42:49.245
allows the child to have

00:42:49.246 --> 00:42:51.645
some autonomy over
their learning and figure out,

00:42:51.646 --> 00:42:53.805
''Oh, what am I not
thinking about?''

00:42:53.806 --> 00:42:57.320
So, competence, overconfidence.

00:42:57.321 --> 00:42:59.435
One of the ways we
can do that is by

00:42:59.436 --> 00:43:02.275
praising them for
the process part,

00:43:02.276 --> 00:43:03.815
talking a lot about the process

00:43:03.816 --> 00:43:05.745
as opposed to the end product.

00:43:05.746 --> 00:43:07.905
Carol Dweck's research
is really interesting.

00:43:07.906 --> 00:43:09.300
Here's a quick primer.

00:43:09.301 --> 00:43:12.520
She gives a math quiz
to everyone.

00:43:12.521 --> 00:43:14.345
Lets say, this is
a math quiz for all of you,

00:43:14.346 --> 00:43:15.665
and it's right in
your sweet spot.

00:43:15.666 --> 00:43:17.485
It's not going to be
too hard or too easy.

00:43:17.486 --> 00:43:20.225
To half of you, she comes
back and her praise is,

00:43:20.226 --> 00:43:21.975
''Oh sweetie, you got
an eight out of 10.

00:43:21.976 --> 00:43:23.830
You must be so smart.''

00:43:23.831 --> 00:43:25.175
And then, the other
half she says,

00:43:25.176 --> 00:43:26.295
''Oh, you got eight out of 10.

00:43:26.296 --> 00:43:28.120
You must have worked so hard.''

00:43:28.121 --> 00:43:29.570
And then she gives all of

00:43:29.571 --> 00:43:32.650
the kids a slightly harder quiz.

00:43:32.651 --> 00:43:34.295
And when the results come in,

00:43:34.296 --> 00:43:36.225
the kids who had been
praised for how smart they

00:43:36.226 --> 00:43:38.185
are do the same or
a little bit worse,

00:43:38.186 --> 00:43:40.385
which it's a harder
quiz of course.

00:43:40.386 --> 00:43:42.425
The kids who've been praised
for how hard they worked

00:43:42.426 --> 00:43:44.425
do the same or a
little bit better,

00:43:44.426 --> 00:43:46.220
which is interesting
to me as a teacher,

00:43:46.221 --> 00:43:48.545
but not as interesting as this.

00:43:48.546 --> 00:43:50.230
Because then she
says, ''Thank you

00:43:50.231 --> 00:43:52.625
so much for being
a part of this today.

00:43:52.626 --> 00:43:54.445
Now, before you go,

00:43:54.446 --> 00:43:58.205
I have these challenge
questions. They're hard.

00:43:58.206 --> 00:44:00.035
You may not be able
to do all of them.

00:44:00.036 --> 00:44:01.900
Who want some?''

00:44:01.901 --> 00:44:05.180
The kids who were praised
for how smart they are want

00:44:05.181 --> 00:44:08.200
nothing to do with
the challenge problems.

00:44:08.201 --> 00:44:10.065
However, the kids who
were praised for how

00:44:10.066 --> 00:44:12.165
hard they worked want
the challenge problems.

00:44:12.166 --> 00:44:13.825
In fact, one girl in
the experiment said,

00:44:13.826 --> 00:44:15.395
''well, of course, we want
the challenge problems.

00:44:15.396 --> 00:44:16.505
Those are the fun
ones. Those are

00:44:16.506 --> 00:44:17.950
the ones we have to work out.''

00:44:17.951 --> 00:44:20.205
Again, I come back
to this discussion

00:44:20.206 --> 00:44:21.790
of desirable difficulties.

00:44:21.791 --> 00:44:24.125
If you people don't want
the challenge problems,

00:44:24.126 --> 00:44:25.855
you're probably not stretching

00:44:25.856 --> 00:44:27.680
yourself and learning as well.

00:44:27.681 --> 00:44:30.040
So that's interesting.
But again,

00:44:30.041 --> 00:44:32.305
I'm really really interested
in how we can create

00:44:32.306 --> 00:44:34.975
learning environments that
are not conducive to cheating,

00:44:34.976 --> 00:44:37.075
that kids don't feel like
they have to cheat in.

00:44:37.076 --> 00:44:38.840
I'm really fascinated by

00:44:38.841 --> 00:44:42.485
academic dishonesty and
how we can fix that.

00:44:42.486 --> 00:44:44.755
Here's what I love,
is then I say,

00:44:44.756 --> 00:44:46.095
Ms. Carol Dweck says,

00:44:46.096 --> 00:44:48.595
I love pretending to be
Carol Dweck, she says, ''Now,

00:44:48.596 --> 00:44:50.875
before you go, if

00:44:50.876 --> 00:44:53.035
everyone could flip over
their piece of paper,

00:44:53.036 --> 00:44:55.050
I'm going to give this quiz
at another school.

00:44:55.051 --> 00:44:56.775
And if you could flip
over your piece of paper

00:44:56.776 --> 00:44:58.375
and write down your experience

00:44:58.376 --> 00:45:02.065
of taking this quiz and
what your score was,

00:45:02.066 --> 00:45:04.690
that would be really helpful
for those other kids."

00:45:04.691 --> 00:45:07.475
You people over here who
were praised for your effort,

00:45:07.476 --> 00:45:09.745
how hard you worked,
you wrote down

00:45:09.746 --> 00:45:13.120
your experience and you
wrote down your quiz score.

00:45:13.121 --> 00:45:15.875
Almost 50 percent of you

00:45:15.876 --> 00:45:18.255
people who were praised
for how smart you are,

00:45:18.256 --> 00:45:22.945
lied about your score and
artificially inflated it.

00:45:22.946 --> 00:45:24.720
When I ask kids,

00:45:24.721 --> 00:45:26.485
again with those six graders
but I've asked

00:45:26.486 --> 00:45:28.380
this question to kids as
young as fourth-grade,

00:45:28.381 --> 00:45:29.685
"Why do you think the kids who

00:45:29.686 --> 00:45:31.055
were praised for being smart,

00:45:31.056 --> 00:45:33.100
don't want the
challenge problems

00:45:33.101 --> 00:45:34.825
and lie about their scores?"

00:45:34.826 --> 00:45:36.630
And they're like, "Well, dah,

00:45:36.631 --> 00:45:38.505
if you've told us
that you're smart,

00:45:38.506 --> 00:45:40.065
we're not going
to screw that up.

00:45:40.066 --> 00:45:42.330
We need for you to
keep believing that.

00:45:42.331 --> 00:45:43.615
We need the kids around us,

00:45:43.616 --> 00:45:45.180
our friends to keep
believing that,

00:45:45.181 --> 00:45:47.605
and that means that
everything has to look easy,

00:45:47.606 --> 00:45:48.705
and if we take the challenge

00:45:48.706 --> 00:45:50.115
problems we're probably going to

00:45:50.116 --> 00:45:51.355
get some wrong and you're

00:45:51.356 --> 00:45:53.315
not going to think
we're smart anymore,

00:45:53.316 --> 00:45:55.445
and if other people are

00:45:55.446 --> 00:45:57.310
going to know about
our quizzes, well,

00:45:57.311 --> 00:45:59.015
we're going to lie
a little bit and

00:45:59.016 --> 00:46:01.875
say that we got
a higher score than we did".

00:46:01.876 --> 00:46:03.775
James M. Lang, who wrote a book

00:46:03.776 --> 00:46:05.965
called Cheating Lessons about

00:46:05.966 --> 00:46:08.175
creating learning environments
where kids don't

00:46:08.176 --> 00:46:10.650
need to cheat said,
"It's really clear.

00:46:10.651 --> 00:46:13.325
If you want to create
a classroom full of cheaters,

00:46:13.326 --> 00:46:15.895
just keep telling them
how smart they are."

00:46:15.896 --> 00:46:17.865
I don't think it's
quite that simple,

00:46:17.866 --> 00:46:19.385
the rest of the book
goes on to explain

00:46:19.386 --> 00:46:20.545
how the way we assess

00:46:20.546 --> 00:46:23.545
kids right now is probably
not the best way to do it.

00:46:23.546 --> 00:46:25.235
And again, I don't
have time to talk

00:46:25.236 --> 00:46:26.975
about how I feel about grading,

00:46:26.976 --> 00:46:29.435
and how I feel about
various forms of assessment,

00:46:29.436 --> 00:46:31.485
I've written about
it extensively at

00:46:31.486 --> 00:46:33.870
the Atlantic and the New York
Times, you can go find it.

00:46:33.871 --> 00:46:36.105
And the piece of
the Atlantic is called Why

00:46:36.106 --> 00:46:38.455
Letter Grades
Deserve an F. Turns

00:46:38.456 --> 00:46:40.965
out the way we test

00:46:40.966 --> 00:46:44.105
kids doesn't tend to work
very well for learning anyway.

00:46:44.106 --> 00:46:50.010
Okay. Autonomy,
competence, connection.

00:46:50.011 --> 00:46:53.115
Again for teachers, this
all comes down to relevance,

00:46:53.116 --> 00:46:55.400
this all comes down
that teacher whose,

00:46:55.401 --> 00:46:57.375
you know sometimes
these teachers have these,

00:46:57.376 --> 00:46:58.685
you can't put your finger on it,

00:46:58.686 --> 00:46:59.995
you don't know
what exactly it is

00:46:59.996 --> 00:47:01.715
but they make
learning come alive.

00:47:01.716 --> 00:47:03.265
That teacher who
comes in and says,

00:47:03.266 --> 00:47:05.085
"I found out
last night that there

00:47:05.086 --> 00:47:07.060
was an earthquake in Peru,

00:47:07.061 --> 00:47:09.085
and so let me show you
some pictures of the bridges

00:47:09.086 --> 00:47:11.165
that fell down and
the bridges that stayed up,

00:47:11.166 --> 00:47:13.845
and let's see what we can

00:47:13.846 --> 00:47:15.135
gather from looking at

00:47:15.136 --> 00:47:17.205
these two different
kinds of bridges.

00:47:17.206 --> 00:47:20.065
And okay, here's some bridges
over here that fell down

00:47:20.066 --> 00:47:23.085
and oh my gosh,
they're all rectangles.

00:47:23.086 --> 00:47:25.685
And look at these bridges
over here that are arches.

00:47:25.686 --> 00:47:27.155
Those are still standing.

00:47:27.156 --> 00:47:29.285
And this is geometry class.

00:47:29.286 --> 00:47:31.025
And did you know that
the people who built

00:47:31.026 --> 00:47:34.085
those bridges were
once kids just like you

00:47:34.086 --> 00:47:36.275
who sat in these
geometry classes and

00:47:36.276 --> 00:47:39.125
found out about things
like the Roman aqueducts,

00:47:39.126 --> 00:47:41.560
where the arches are what
have kept them standing for

00:47:41.561 --> 00:47:45.370
thousands of years and why
this kind of bridge over here,

00:47:45.371 --> 00:47:47.020
is likely not to work?"

00:47:47.021 --> 00:47:49.710
And those kids not
only learn about

00:47:49.711 --> 00:47:53.370
shapes that work in geometry
and why those shapes work,

00:47:53.371 --> 00:47:55.150
but what they also learn is,

00:47:55.151 --> 00:47:57.475
"Oh my gosh, I could do that.

00:47:57.476 --> 00:47:58.845
I could build bridges that

00:47:58.846 --> 00:48:01.105
will hold up when
there's an earthquake".

00:48:01.106 --> 00:48:04.770
That's called self-efficacy.

00:48:04.771 --> 00:48:08.780
Self-efficacy is an incredibly
important element of

00:48:08.781 --> 00:48:11.475
any education program
which is knowing that

00:48:11.476 --> 00:48:12.635
not only do you have

00:48:12.636 --> 00:48:14.825
the ability to change
your own life too,

00:48:14.826 --> 00:48:17.535
that the decisions you make
will make a difference.

00:48:17.536 --> 00:48:19.230
It's why kids who

00:48:19.231 --> 00:48:22.355
are in really traumatic
situations as children,

00:48:22.356 --> 00:48:23.785
children who live in abuse,

00:48:23.786 --> 00:48:24.945
children who live with

00:48:24.946 --> 00:48:27.280
people who are drug
and alcohol addicted,

00:48:27.281 --> 00:48:29.685
they learn that there's not

00:48:29.686 --> 00:48:31.865
a lot they can do to
change their environment.

00:48:31.866 --> 00:48:32.985
They lose their sense of

00:48:32.986 --> 00:48:35.440
self-efficacy and
that's a disaster.

00:48:35.441 --> 00:48:37.875
Not just for their lives
but for their learning.

00:48:37.876 --> 00:48:40.465
It's one of the many reasons
that kids who have

00:48:40.466 --> 00:48:43.475
experienced trauma
at a young age,

00:48:43.476 --> 00:48:45.475
just don't learn as well.

00:48:45.476 --> 00:48:46.665
And not only that,
one of the things

00:48:46.666 --> 00:48:47.800
we know about learning is that

00:48:47.801 --> 00:48:49.860
the very fastest way to

00:48:49.861 --> 00:48:52.690
interrupt learning is
to introduce stress.

00:48:52.691 --> 00:48:54.555
It's why often at the end of

00:48:54.556 --> 00:48:56.245
these talks I'll
get a question about

00:48:56.246 --> 00:48:59.695
the impact of competition
on children and learning,

00:48:59.696 --> 00:49:02.375
and I have to say that's
a very complicated question

00:49:02.376 --> 00:49:05.280
because it turns out for
some kids, it's helpful.

00:49:05.281 --> 00:49:07.775
Competition for
some kids it's fantastic.

00:49:07.776 --> 00:49:09.830
It can lead to better learning,

00:49:09.831 --> 00:49:11.460
can lead to higher achievement.

00:49:11.461 --> 00:49:14.105
But for some kids, it's a
disaster because you could have

00:49:14.106 --> 00:49:17.615
a kid who knows their
multiplication tables really,

00:49:17.616 --> 00:49:19.870
really well, and
the second you say,

00:49:19.871 --> 00:49:24.600
"This is a timed
worksheet", they fall apart.

00:49:24.601 --> 00:49:26.165
It's why Joe Bowler who wrote

00:49:26.166 --> 00:49:28.415
the book Mathematical
Mindsets says,

00:49:28.416 --> 00:49:30.260
the very first thing
we need to do if we

00:49:30.261 --> 00:49:32.750
really want to teach Math
effectively in this country,

00:49:32.751 --> 00:49:35.055
is stop timing worksheets

00:49:35.056 --> 00:49:37.275
because mastery and
being able to do Math

00:49:37.276 --> 00:49:39.385
fast are not the same thing

00:49:39.386 --> 00:49:41.790
and it creates a lot
of Math anxiety

00:49:41.791 --> 00:49:44.245
and that comes as
a person who had for much

00:49:44.246 --> 00:49:47.110
of my life really,

00:49:47.111 --> 00:49:48.705
really horrible Math anxiety.

00:49:48.706 --> 00:49:52.435
I was told in seventh grade
that I wasn't a Math person.

00:49:52.445 --> 00:49:56.135
So, when I went back to take
algebra one again when I was

00:49:56.136 --> 00:49:59.645
45 because one of my friends
was teaching the class,

00:49:59.646 --> 00:50:01.475
and it was right next
to where my office was,

00:50:01.476 --> 00:50:02.935
and I told you about all that

00:50:02.936 --> 00:50:04.665
free time I had when I was
a middle school teacher.

00:50:04.666 --> 00:50:06.835
Yeah. I tossed
that one prep period

00:50:06.836 --> 00:50:07.975
and I sat in onto her class

00:50:07.976 --> 00:50:09.425
and I took algebra one at

00:50:09.426 --> 00:50:11.405
the same time my older son
took algebra one.

00:50:11.406 --> 00:50:14.075
We took it together. And I
took it with my students.

00:50:14.076 --> 00:50:17.525
And I learned that
I'm not bad at Math,

00:50:17.526 --> 00:50:20.945
Math is hard. Math
is challenging.

00:50:20.946 --> 00:50:22.185
And so, one of the things that

00:50:22.186 --> 00:50:23.495
happens in this competence,

00:50:23.496 --> 00:50:25.755
confidence area is,
we have these kids,

00:50:25.756 --> 00:50:27.930
we tell them early
on in their lives,

00:50:27.931 --> 00:50:30.735
"You were born good at Math.

00:50:30.736 --> 00:50:33.100
You're just so great at Math."

00:50:33.101 --> 00:50:35.865
And what happens to
these kids is that they

00:50:35.866 --> 00:50:36.995
start to believe that it's

00:50:36.996 --> 00:50:38.620
always supposed to
be easy for them,

00:50:38.621 --> 00:50:40.535
because if it's
not easy for them,

00:50:40.536 --> 00:50:42.045
then maybe they aren't as

00:50:42.046 --> 00:50:44.310
smart as we're
telling them they are.

00:50:44.311 --> 00:50:49.145
When I went back and retook
algebra one in my 40s,

00:50:49.146 --> 00:50:51.175
my mentor for that
was Steven Strogatz,

00:50:51.176 --> 00:50:52.930
who's a Math professor
at Cornell.

00:50:52.931 --> 00:50:54.895
And he says, one of
the things he says is,

00:50:54.896 --> 00:50:58.095
we got to stop telling kids
that they're just good at

00:50:58.096 --> 00:51:02.855
Math because Math will get
hard for everyone otherwise,

00:51:02.856 --> 00:51:07.065
we wouldn't have proofs that
we can't solve out there.

00:51:07.066 --> 00:51:08.645
We wouldn't have had
Fermat's Last Theorem

00:51:08.646 --> 00:51:11.050
sitting around for
as long as we did.

00:51:11.051 --> 00:51:13.575
For those kids who are

00:51:13.576 --> 00:51:15.615
told that they're
just good at Math,

00:51:15.616 --> 00:51:17.575
they're going to be just good
at Math for a little while,

00:51:17.576 --> 00:51:18.685
and it's going to be
going great and they're

00:51:18.686 --> 00:51:19.945
going to be doing
really, really well,

00:51:19.946 --> 00:51:21.585
and then it's going
to get hard for

00:51:21.586 --> 00:51:23.485
them whether that's
in sixth grade,

00:51:23.486 --> 00:51:25.865
or tenth grade, or
freshman year of college,

00:51:25.866 --> 00:51:28.605
it just happens
to a lot of kids.

00:51:28.606 --> 00:51:31.225
How they respond to
that when it does

00:51:31.226 --> 00:51:34.075
get challenging is everything.

00:51:34.076 --> 00:51:37.770
That determines how well
they're going to do over time.

00:51:37.771 --> 00:51:40.265
There's a lot of
kids actually who

00:51:40.266 --> 00:51:42.810
hit that moment and
just drop it and say,

00:51:42.811 --> 00:51:45.955
"Oh, this was
all Impostor Syndrome.

00:51:45.956 --> 00:51:47.445
This was all a big mistake and I

00:51:47.446 --> 00:51:49.565
wasn't really ever as
smart as I thought I was."

00:51:49.566 --> 00:51:51.665
So for teachers,
this relevant stuff is

00:51:51.666 --> 00:51:53.645
like multilayered, and amazing.

00:51:53.646 --> 00:51:54.735
And again, if your kids have

00:51:54.736 --> 00:51:55.975
teachers that are
making learning

00:51:55.976 --> 00:51:57.150
relevant and making kids

00:51:57.151 --> 00:51:59.250
feel like they
have self-efficacy,

00:51:59.251 --> 00:52:00.585
oh love those teachers up.

00:52:00.586 --> 00:52:01.750
They're just fantastic.

00:52:01.751 --> 00:52:04.320
For parents it's
really, really simple.

00:52:04.321 --> 00:52:07.925
Connection for parents
comes down to two things.

00:52:07.926 --> 00:52:12.505
You have to love
the child you have,

00:52:12.506 --> 00:52:15.060
not the child you wish you had.

00:52:15.061 --> 00:52:18.025
Not some fantasy of
the child that you

00:52:18.026 --> 00:52:19.545
developed based
on all the things

00:52:19.546 --> 00:52:20.895
you wish you had done, "Oh,

00:52:20.896 --> 00:52:23.175
if I had just practiced piano,

00:52:23.176 --> 00:52:24.575
I could be so good now, so,

00:52:24.576 --> 00:52:27.690
I'm going to sit on my kid
and make them practice piano."

00:52:27.691 --> 00:52:29.365
All of those fantasies you have

00:52:29.366 --> 00:52:31.030
about who your child could be,

00:52:31.031 --> 00:52:32.865
may not be the child

00:52:32.866 --> 00:52:34.170
that's sitting right
in front of you.

00:52:34.171 --> 00:52:38.225
And our kids know it when
we're not loving them,

00:52:38.226 --> 00:52:40.950
when we don't see them.

00:52:40.951 --> 00:52:44.525
So, love the kid you have
not the kid you wish you had,

00:52:44.526 --> 00:52:48.555
and don't just love your kids
based on their performance.

00:52:48.556 --> 00:52:50.045
And I know, I'm like,

00:52:50.046 --> 00:52:53.125
"I would never do
that except when they

00:52:53.126 --> 00:52:54.885
come home with a test

00:52:54.886 --> 00:52:56.340
that they're really
excited to show us",

00:52:56.341 --> 00:52:58.172
they're like, look what.

00:52:58.173 --> 00:53:01.690
Show it to us and it's
an A and we flipout,

00:53:01.691 --> 00:53:04.430
and were like put it on
Instagram and put it on

00:53:04.431 --> 00:53:07.780
Facebook and let's live
tweet and move, move, move.

00:53:07.781 --> 00:53:09.130
And you freak out.

00:53:09.131 --> 00:53:10.680
They're getting
the message that,

00:53:10.681 --> 00:53:11.760
oh here we go,

00:53:11.761 --> 00:53:13.760
I've given them the good stuff,
the stuff they love.

00:53:13.761 --> 00:53:19.685
But then they come home a
little more sedate with the F,

00:53:19.686 --> 00:53:22.185
I'm sorry, B minus, that's
what we call an F now, right?

00:53:22.186 --> 00:53:26.610
Yeah. Okay, with that F
or B minus and we show it,

00:53:26.611 --> 00:53:29.780
they show it to us
and we're silent.

00:53:29.781 --> 00:53:32.340
That silence, by the way,
when I talk to kids,

00:53:32.341 --> 00:53:34.500
I make it really clear
that this parenting thing

00:53:34.501 --> 00:53:37.045
is so important to us
that we have to like,

00:53:37.046 --> 00:53:38.850
look for just the right words

00:53:38.851 --> 00:53:40.150
to say just the right thing.

00:53:40.151 --> 00:53:43.550
But ask any psychologist,
that silence,

00:53:43.551 --> 00:53:46.255
that's withdrawal
of love based on

00:53:46.256 --> 00:53:47.530
performance and it's one of

00:53:47.531 --> 00:53:48.780
the most emotionally devastating

00:53:48.781 --> 00:53:50.015
things we can do to kids.

00:53:50.016 --> 00:53:53.495
Unless you think that you
would never like I did,

00:53:53.496 --> 00:53:55.960
remember when I
ask those kids how

00:53:55.961 --> 00:53:58.630
many of you believe that
your parents love you

00:53:58.631 --> 00:54:02.215
more when you bring them
the grades and scores that

00:54:02.216 --> 00:54:03.920
they want versus the ones they

00:54:03.921 --> 00:54:06.370
don't and you get
those 80 to 90 percent.

00:54:06.371 --> 00:54:07.600
I mean, I think it's lower

00:54:07.601 --> 00:54:08.850
for middle school
because I think

00:54:08.851 --> 00:54:12.085
middle school kids still
have some faith in us.

00:54:12.086 --> 00:54:13.480
They're still like oh,

00:54:13.481 --> 00:54:16.430
they're trying to do
the right thing I can tell.

00:54:16.431 --> 00:54:18.440
And it's the reason
also that the heart of

00:54:18.441 --> 00:54:21.015
the gift of failure is
on middle school because

00:54:21.016 --> 00:54:23.525
really that's
our moment to really

00:54:23.526 --> 00:54:25.280
allow them to make mistakes and

00:54:25.281 --> 00:54:27.265
allow them to learn
from those mistakes.

00:54:27.266 --> 00:54:31.820
So autonomy, competence
and connection.

00:54:31.821 --> 00:54:33.480
Those are the three pieces that

00:54:33.481 --> 00:54:36.030
we need to bring
together in some way.

00:54:36.031 --> 00:54:38.560
And I don't want to go
too long today and I want

00:54:38.561 --> 00:54:40.735
to leave plenty of
time for questions,

00:54:40.736 --> 00:54:44.905
but bringing that together
is a really amazing thing.

00:54:44.906 --> 00:54:47.320
I'm so fortunate I get

00:54:47.321 --> 00:54:50.495
a lot of letters from parents
and I love all of them.

00:54:50.496 --> 00:54:54.300
And most of them say things
like, "Oh you're so right.

00:54:54.301 --> 00:54:56.690
I let my kid you know
put her dishes in

00:54:56.691 --> 00:54:58.500
the dishwasher and
she was so proud of

00:54:58.501 --> 00:55:00.535
herself and she's becoming
more and more competent."

00:55:00.536 --> 00:55:03.105
And I love those
letters. I love them.

00:55:03.106 --> 00:55:05.170
But I love these even more,

00:55:05.171 --> 00:55:06.560
the ones that I get that says,

00:55:06.561 --> 00:55:09.610
"You know what, I gave
my kid more autonomy."

00:55:09.611 --> 00:55:11.110
I sort of said,
"You know Sweetie,

00:55:11.111 --> 00:55:13.570
I think I've been
doing too much for you,

00:55:13.571 --> 00:55:15.850
and I actually think

00:55:15.851 --> 00:55:18.770
you're able to do much more
than I give you credit for.

00:55:18.771 --> 00:55:20.970
So, this is now going
to be your thing."

00:55:20.971 --> 00:55:22.080
And usually that's homework.

00:55:22.081 --> 00:55:23.665
Homework is
a good place to start.

00:55:23.666 --> 00:55:24.860
"This is your thing now.

00:55:24.861 --> 00:55:26.445
I'm not going to be
checking the portal.

00:55:26.446 --> 00:55:27.620
I'm not going to be sitting

00:55:27.621 --> 00:55:28.840
right there with
you while you do

00:55:28.841 --> 00:55:29.980
your homework because I

00:55:29.981 --> 00:55:31.440
have faith that
this is your thing."

00:55:31.441 --> 00:55:33.030
And then talking really,

00:55:33.031 --> 00:55:35.125
really clearly about
your expectations

00:55:35.126 --> 00:55:37.015
and your consequences
around homework.

00:55:37.016 --> 00:55:38.990
Expectations could be you know,

00:55:38.991 --> 00:55:42.030
for us was for a long time
it was just you'll do

00:55:42.031 --> 00:55:43.560
your homework until we realize

00:55:43.561 --> 00:55:45.650
that he was doing his homework.

00:55:45.651 --> 00:55:46.610
This is Phin I'm talking

00:55:46.611 --> 00:55:47.880
about because his story
is in the book.

00:55:47.881 --> 00:55:49.030
He was doing his homework,

00:55:49.031 --> 00:55:51.705
he just wasn't getting
the homework into

00:55:51.706 --> 00:55:56.430
the backpack and taking the
homework out of the backpack.

00:55:56.431 --> 00:55:57.500
So, this was, you know,

00:55:57.501 --> 00:56:00.730
our expectations and
our strategies had to shift.

00:56:00.731 --> 00:56:03.640
And then making
the consequences really,

00:56:03.641 --> 00:56:05.630
really clear about what
will happen if they're

00:56:05.631 --> 00:56:07.810
not meeting those expectations.

00:56:07.811 --> 00:56:09.220
And by the way our consequences

00:56:09.221 --> 00:56:10.340
that we tend to come up with as

00:56:10.341 --> 00:56:12.490
adults make perfect sense to us

00:56:12.491 --> 00:56:15.270
sometimes or don't
make any sense to kids.

00:56:15.271 --> 00:56:18.470
Kids, younger kids in
particular, their frontal lobe,

00:56:18.471 --> 00:56:19.950
our frontal lobe
is the last part

00:56:19.951 --> 00:56:21.565
of our brain to fully cook.

00:56:21.566 --> 00:56:24.480
And that's where all
the organization,

00:56:24.481 --> 00:56:27.900
the executive function,
time management,

00:56:27.901 --> 00:56:29.890
organization of all that stuff,

00:56:29.891 --> 00:56:31.580
the locker and the classes,

00:56:31.581 --> 00:56:32.910
and the notebooks and keeping

00:56:32.911 --> 00:56:34.810
the pants up and
tying the shoes,

00:56:34.811 --> 00:56:37.210
all that stuff happens
in executive function.

00:56:37.211 --> 00:56:39.980
And executive function in
our frontal lobe is not fully

00:56:39.981 --> 00:56:43.215
cooked until we're
in our early 20s.

00:56:43.216 --> 00:56:45.160
I used to say, "Yeah,

00:56:45.161 --> 00:56:47.810
you can just wait until
your kids brain is fully baked."

00:56:47.811 --> 00:56:49.160
And then I realized that

00:56:49.161 --> 00:56:50.840
cooked maybe works
better because fully

00:56:50.841 --> 00:56:54.445
baked just has the wrong
connotation about it.

00:56:54.446 --> 00:56:56.065
But waiting until their brain,

00:56:56.066 --> 00:56:57.940
sometimes there are just places

00:56:57.941 --> 00:56:59.110
in life like middle
school where we're

00:56:59.111 --> 00:57:00.790
asking them to do way more than

00:57:00.791 --> 00:57:02.890
their brain can actually
handle which is,

00:57:02.891 --> 00:57:04.780
as I come back to the magic

00:57:04.781 --> 00:57:06.410
of middle school
because my job is to

00:57:06.411 --> 00:57:08.100
watch kids grow up
all day long and

00:57:08.101 --> 00:57:10.115
help them come up with
strategies to fix them.

00:57:10.116 --> 00:57:13.190
And anytime those
moments are stolen,

00:57:13.191 --> 00:57:16.105
that's really difficult
for them and for us.

00:57:16.106 --> 00:57:19.720
Autonomy, competence,
connection, because

00:57:19.721 --> 00:57:21.850
that connection part
here is what can happen.

00:57:21.851 --> 00:57:23.935
When we do give
our kids more autonomy,

00:57:23.936 --> 00:57:26.300
let's say around
homework, and we back off,

00:57:26.301 --> 00:57:28.860
the letters then that I get are

00:57:28.861 --> 00:57:31.440
not just my kid has become
more competent which I love,

00:57:31.441 --> 00:57:34.345
but our relationship
has improved.

00:57:34.346 --> 00:57:36.740
We talk about stuff
that matters not

00:57:36.741 --> 00:57:39.255
just the stuff that
I want to talk about.

00:57:39.256 --> 00:57:43.495
When I sat in recently
on a panel of freshmen,

00:57:43.496 --> 00:57:45.810
sorry, seniors in
high school who were giving

00:57:45.811 --> 00:57:48.380
advice to parents of
incoming freshmen.

00:57:48.381 --> 00:57:50.425
One of the parents said,
"What's the biggest,

00:57:50.426 --> 00:57:52.860
the most important piece
of advice you could give us

00:57:52.861 --> 00:57:53.990
about how to help our kids have

00:57:53.991 --> 00:57:55.540
a good first year
in high school?"

00:57:55.541 --> 00:57:57.945
And the kids said, "Stop

00:57:57.946 --> 00:58:01.445
talking to them about grades
every minute of the day.

00:58:01.446 --> 00:58:04.155
They know grades are important."

00:58:04.156 --> 00:58:05.820
Maybe, and in the book I talk

00:58:05.821 --> 00:58:07.430
about a possible alternative,

00:58:07.431 --> 00:58:09.515
maybe we could talk to
them about their goals.

00:58:09.516 --> 00:58:11.420
Maybe we could talk to
them about what they love,

00:58:11.421 --> 00:58:12.650
what they're interested in.

00:58:12.651 --> 00:58:14.000
Talk to them about what their

00:58:14.001 --> 00:58:15.440
personal goals are for school as

00:58:15.441 --> 00:58:18.245
opposed to what
their grades are that day.

00:58:18.246 --> 00:58:19.960
Because then, when you get into

00:58:19.961 --> 00:58:22.460
that stuff that's when you
get to really know your kids.

00:58:22.461 --> 00:58:24.895
You get to know the kid
that's in front of you.

00:58:24.896 --> 00:58:26.220
The kid, your kid,

00:58:26.221 --> 00:58:29.050
not that fantasy kid
you wish you had.

00:58:29.051 --> 00:58:30.950
That's what sort of allows

00:58:30.951 --> 00:58:33.050
kids to feel like they
can trust us to have

00:58:33.051 --> 00:58:34.610
conversations that
are a little bit

00:58:34.611 --> 00:58:37.085
difficult about maybe
where they screwed up,

00:58:37.086 --> 00:58:38.650
because they know
that we're not just

00:58:38.651 --> 00:58:39.960
loving them based
on performance,

00:58:39.961 --> 00:58:41.090
we're loving them because we

00:58:41.091 --> 00:58:45.180
love them and that
we support them.

00:58:45.440 --> 00:58:47.570
All right, I'm
going to stop there

00:58:47.571 --> 00:58:49.220
because I could keep
going on for hours,

00:58:49.221 --> 00:58:50.490
but I'm assuming
there are questions,

00:58:50.491 --> 00:58:51.970
I happen to know there
are some questions

00:58:51.971 --> 00:58:53.740
before I even came into this.

00:58:53.741 --> 00:58:56.840
On YouTube, I have a series
called Gift to Failure Facts.

00:58:56.841 --> 00:58:58.245
A frequently asked questions,

00:58:58.246 --> 00:59:02.500
answering the most common
questions I get via email.

00:59:02.501 --> 00:59:07.250
So, they're like how to help
kids who coast, how to help,

00:59:07.251 --> 00:59:09.390
parent perfectionist kids,

00:59:09.391 --> 00:59:11.310
what about my special needs kid,

00:59:11.311 --> 00:59:13.235
is there a different
message there?

00:59:13.236 --> 00:59:16.470
Should I feel stupid for
trusting the school when they

00:59:16.471 --> 00:59:18.040
told me what I really
need to focus on

00:59:18.041 --> 00:59:20.015
his grades and points in scores.

00:59:20.016 --> 00:59:21.730
Really, it's called,
should I feel

00:59:21.731 --> 00:59:24.760
stupid for being duped
by a flawed system.

00:59:24.761 --> 00:59:26.480
There are a lot of flaws with

00:59:26.481 --> 00:59:27.855
our education system right now

00:59:27.856 --> 00:59:30.890
and I couldn't write a book
that appended all of that,

00:59:30.891 --> 00:59:32.750
I had to say, "Here's
what we've got right now,

00:59:32.751 --> 00:59:34.115
how do we work within that?"

00:59:34.116 --> 00:59:36.130
So, focusing for example,

00:59:36.131 --> 00:59:39.500
on goals as opposed to grades
is a great place to start,

00:59:39.501 --> 00:59:42.660
but I'm happy to
take any questions.

00:59:42.790 --> 00:59:45.240
What if I told you I
know what your questions

00:59:45.241 --> 00:59:46.590
are and hopefully
you can ask them.

00:59:46.591 --> 00:59:48.970
Someone's going to ask me
about musical instruments and

00:59:48.971 --> 00:59:50.130
when they are allowed
to let their kid

00:59:50.131 --> 00:59:51.230
quit that's one too.

00:59:51.231 --> 00:59:52.555
I get that one a lot.

00:59:52.556 --> 00:59:54.940
My kid has been playing
soccer since she was

00:59:54.941 --> 00:59:57.360
six and now she's 14
and she's really really,

00:59:57.361 --> 00:59:58.670
really good but she
says she doesn't

00:59:58.671 --> 01:00:00.170
want to play soccer
anymore, what should I do?

01:00:00.171 --> 01:00:02.675
Should I let her quit? That's
a question I get a lot.

01:00:02.676 --> 01:00:04.895
&gt;&gt; What's the answer?

01:00:04.896 --> 01:00:06.840
What's the answer?

01:00:06.841 --> 01:00:08.820
Honestly, certain things
we're talking about

01:00:08.821 --> 01:00:10.380
whether that's music
or athletics really

01:00:10.381 --> 01:00:12.515
depends on your
priorities as a family.

01:00:12.516 --> 01:00:14.410
Often this question
is about sports or

01:00:14.411 --> 01:00:16.610
music and so I'll use
those two examples.

01:00:16.611 --> 01:00:19.055
In my home, we're not,

01:00:19.056 --> 01:00:22.875
we like music but we are not
an intensely musical family.

01:00:22.876 --> 01:00:25.840
A friend of mine who has
a very musical family it is

01:00:25.841 --> 01:00:27.840
a non-negotiable that
their children will play

01:00:27.841 --> 01:00:30.110
some instrument
until they are 18,

01:00:30.111 --> 01:00:31.390
that's just a non-negotiable.

01:00:31.391 --> 01:00:32.630
It's not going any other

01:00:32.631 --> 01:00:34.650
way so for them
quitting an instrument

01:00:34.651 --> 01:00:36.225
means fine what are
you going to take up

01:00:36.226 --> 01:00:38.445
next at which point
the child says,

01:00:38.446 --> 01:00:40.310
"No, I've already
been doing the cello

01:00:40.311 --> 01:00:42.345
for a while I guess I'll
just stick with that."

01:00:42.346 --> 01:00:44.695
In our house, I
tell a story often.

01:00:44.696 --> 01:00:47.760
My son, his older brother.

01:00:47.761 --> 01:00:49.720
Our next door neighbor had

01:00:49.721 --> 01:00:51.465
a piano that they were
getting rid of and I'm like

01:00:51.466 --> 01:00:53.430
perfect because I've read

01:00:53.431 --> 01:00:55.540
the research showing that if
your kid could do music that

01:00:55.541 --> 01:00:57.670
they're going to be better
at math and so I really need

01:00:57.671 --> 01:01:00.095
to do the music thing in them
and piano that's perfect.

01:01:00.096 --> 01:01:01.760
And so we wheeled
the piano down from

01:01:01.761 --> 01:01:04.030
their house into
our house and we got him

01:01:04.031 --> 01:01:05.370
lessons and we had

01:01:05.371 --> 01:01:06.680
the honeymoon period where

01:01:06.681 --> 01:01:08.545
it's all great for like a week,

01:01:08.546 --> 01:01:12.225
maybe two weeks and then
it was fighting constantly.

01:01:12.226 --> 01:01:14.310
Constantly you have to practice.

01:01:14.311 --> 01:01:15.350
Here's what you
need to practice.

01:01:15.351 --> 01:01:16.610
How are you going to practice.
What do I need to do?

01:01:16.611 --> 01:01:17.855
Have you practiced yet?

01:01:17.856 --> 01:01:20.590
And it was really undermining
our relationship and for me

01:01:20.591 --> 01:01:24.205
a non-negotiable is
our relationship and so,

01:01:24.206 --> 01:01:27.425
and for me whether or not
he plays piano honestly,

01:01:27.426 --> 01:01:29.445
I could care less
really and truly.

01:01:29.446 --> 01:01:32.770
So we wheeled the piano
down the street to

01:01:32.771 --> 01:01:35.940
the next suckers house and
it can only go downhill

01:01:35.941 --> 01:01:39.610
and we're on a big hill so
it came down and then it

01:01:39.611 --> 01:01:41.590
went down to my friend
Vicky's house and

01:01:41.591 --> 01:01:44.160
then it went down
from their house down.

01:01:44.161 --> 01:01:45.940
But then what was
really interesting

01:01:45.941 --> 01:01:47.255
is a couple of years later,

01:01:47.256 --> 01:01:49.430
my husband plays
guitar and my older son

01:01:49.431 --> 01:01:51.615
saw my husband playing
guitar and said,

01:01:51.616 --> 01:01:53.270
you know I think I might
want to do some guitar

01:01:53.271 --> 01:01:54.830
and so he took some lessons,

01:01:54.831 --> 01:01:56.420
he learned out very
quickly that it wasn't

01:01:56.421 --> 01:01:58.470
the piano or the guitar
that he was not enjoying,

01:01:58.471 --> 01:02:00.205
it was the lesson format.

01:02:00.206 --> 01:02:01.950
He just doesn't
like having someone

01:02:01.951 --> 01:02:03.890
else tell him what he
has to play and when.

01:02:03.891 --> 01:02:05.280
So he doesn't like anyone

01:02:05.281 --> 01:02:06.905
telling him what
he has to do ever.

01:02:06.906 --> 01:02:09.280
So it turns out there's
plenty of ways you

01:02:09.281 --> 01:02:11.620
can learn online by
yourself. There's jam play.

01:02:11.621 --> 01:02:13.240
You pay like 100
and something bucks

01:02:13.241 --> 01:02:14.890
and you get to choose
your instructor and choose

01:02:14.891 --> 01:02:17.310
your lessons and you
can repeat them or fast

01:02:17.311 --> 01:02:18.710
forward or whatever or

01:02:18.711 --> 01:02:20.575
YouTube is another place
you can do that.

01:02:20.576 --> 01:02:22.395
And he became a really,

01:02:22.396 --> 01:02:24.060
really good guitar player and of

01:02:24.061 --> 01:02:25.930
course I kept my hands
way off of that

01:02:25.931 --> 01:02:28.360
because as soon as
my mom stink was on

01:02:28.361 --> 01:02:32.065
any aspect of the guitar
playing, it was over for him.

01:02:32.066 --> 01:02:34.160
The minute I messed
with his autonomy and

01:02:34.161 --> 01:02:37.950
his control over this
learning guitar thing, done.

01:02:37.951 --> 01:02:40.110
I was able to sort of
encourage him to you

01:02:40.111 --> 01:02:41.810
know have you listened to

01:02:41.811 --> 01:02:44.010
Eric Clapton yet? You
know, that kind of stuff.

01:02:44.011 --> 01:02:45.890
And then what was

01:02:45.891 --> 01:02:47.220
really interesting is
after he'd been playing

01:02:47.221 --> 01:02:48.390
guitar for about a year and he

01:02:48.391 --> 01:02:50.530
really was feeling confident,

01:02:50.531 --> 01:02:52.440
he sheepishly came
to me and he said,

01:02:52.441 --> 01:02:53.910
"You know, that list
serve we have in

01:02:53.911 --> 01:02:55.670
town where people use stuff,

01:02:55.671 --> 01:02:57.110
like if anyone had

01:02:57.111 --> 01:03:00.985
a keyboard I might be
interested in learning piano."

01:03:00.986 --> 01:03:03.510
And I was able to find
a keyboard on our list serve,

01:03:03.511 --> 01:03:05.370
I paid like $100
for it and he taught

01:03:05.371 --> 01:03:07.950
himself piano in the same way
he taught himself guitar.

01:03:07.951 --> 01:03:09.870
And I really believe that he's

01:03:09.871 --> 01:03:11.820
a better musician than
he ever would have been

01:03:11.821 --> 01:03:13.300
if I had sat there and forced

01:03:13.301 --> 01:03:17.160
him but that's
priorities in my house.

01:03:17.161 --> 01:03:18.840
Same thing with athletics.

01:03:18.841 --> 01:03:21.610
If you know, if your priority
is sports and you're

01:03:21.611 --> 01:03:24.900
a very sports-oriented family
then you're quitting.

01:03:24.901 --> 01:03:27.010
But then again my
other responses.

01:03:27.011 --> 01:03:28.710
Childhood is supposed
to be a time when we

01:03:28.711 --> 01:03:30.820
get to try lots of
different things.

01:03:30.821 --> 01:03:33.170
In my house, you

01:03:33.171 --> 01:03:35.320
know the kids can take
lessons in whatever they

01:03:35.321 --> 01:03:39.710
want anytime my younger son
just took lessons in welding

01:03:39.711 --> 01:03:41.950
a couple weekends ago and now
thinks he might want to try

01:03:41.951 --> 01:03:43.140
hot and cold forging because

01:03:43.141 --> 01:03:44.420
that could be
really interesting.

01:03:44.421 --> 01:03:45.860
And luckily we have a place

01:03:45.861 --> 01:03:47.910
nearby that will
teach them teens that.

01:03:47.911 --> 01:03:51.720
But if you have a kid who's
14 who every moment there

01:03:51.721 --> 01:03:53.785
is taken up with
playing soccer that

01:03:53.786 --> 01:03:55.950
they'd been playing since
they were six and they really,

01:03:55.951 --> 01:03:57.560
really hate it, what are

01:03:57.561 --> 01:03:59.950
they not getting
an opportunity to try?

01:03:59.951 --> 01:04:02.070
We tend to think that we'll be

01:04:02.071 --> 01:04:03.890
raising quitters if we
let them quit but you

01:04:03.891 --> 01:04:05.430
can't know if you like or don't

01:04:05.431 --> 01:04:07.390
like something until
you have tried it.

01:04:07.391 --> 01:04:09.360
So trying something
and then deciding

01:04:09.361 --> 01:04:11.655
it's not for you
is not quitting.

01:04:11.656 --> 01:04:13.960
That's knowing about yourself

01:04:13.961 --> 01:04:16.460
and going on to something
else that might be more fun.

01:04:16.461 --> 01:04:19.060
And often the answer
I'll get from parents is,

01:04:19.061 --> 01:04:22.490
"But we have so much
invested in soccer."

01:04:22.491 --> 01:04:23.740
And I just want to say,

01:04:23.741 --> 01:04:26.250
"What's the return on this
investment you're hoping for?"

01:04:26.251 --> 01:04:27.750
Like there's
a great New York Times

01:04:27.751 --> 01:04:28.950
headline a while back that said,

01:04:28.951 --> 01:04:30.010
"Your kid is not going to play

01:04:30.011 --> 01:04:32.830
professional sports, now what?"

01:04:32.831 --> 01:04:35.770
Know what your goals
are going into this and

01:04:35.771 --> 01:04:38.385
that's one reason the sports
chapter was so fun to write.

01:04:38.386 --> 01:04:41.190
There was one study
showing that,

01:04:41.191 --> 01:04:42.605
it was a small,

01:04:42.606 --> 01:04:44.170
an informal study but of

01:04:44.171 --> 01:04:45.985
lots of kids where
this guy said,

01:04:45.986 --> 01:04:47.000
"What's your favorite thing

01:04:47.001 --> 01:04:48.010
about youth sports and what was

01:04:48.011 --> 01:04:49.740
your least favorite thing
about youth sports?"

01:04:49.741 --> 01:04:51.870
He asked serious
athletes about this.

01:04:51.871 --> 01:04:54.520
And they said, "My least
favorite thing about

01:04:54.521 --> 01:04:56.320
youth sports was
the ride home in the car

01:04:56.321 --> 01:04:58.635
with my parents after games."

01:04:58.636 --> 01:05:00.910
Now, I don't know
about you, a lot

01:05:00.911 --> 01:05:02.545
of good talking
happens in the car

01:05:02.546 --> 01:05:04.340
and the idea that that would be

01:05:04.341 --> 01:05:07.180
the worst thing about
youth sports gives me headaches.

01:05:07.181 --> 01:05:09.580
And their favorite thing
about youth sports was

01:05:09.581 --> 01:05:12.240
when their grandparents
came to watch them play.

01:05:12.241 --> 01:05:15.300
Because the grandparents
don't care what the score is.

01:05:15.301 --> 01:05:18.230
The grandparents are in
it to watch the kid play.

01:05:18.231 --> 01:05:20.280
One of my favorite
websites out there

01:05:20.281 --> 01:05:22.465
about being a good
sports parent is called

01:05:22.466 --> 01:05:24.600
Ilovetowatchyouplay.com

01:05:24.890 --> 01:05:27.570
because that's
really in the end for

01:05:27.571 --> 01:05:28.770
many kids what it's all about

01:05:28.771 --> 01:05:30.130
and that's why I get
to spend a lot of

01:05:30.131 --> 01:05:32.100
time on sports podcasts

01:05:32.101 --> 01:05:33.310
and things like that.
Did you have a question?

01:05:33.311 --> 01:05:37.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah. As someone who hasn't
read your book as of yet,

01:05:37.181 --> 01:05:38.660
that might be one
of the answers.

01:05:38.661 --> 01:05:39.680
&gt;&gt; Oh I should mention now, from

01:05:39.681 --> 01:05:41.170
whatever not so reason

01:05:41.171 --> 01:05:42.680
and I apologize there
aren't books here today,

01:05:42.681 --> 01:05:44.420
that was a mistake on my part,

01:05:44.421 --> 01:05:49.445
a misunderstanding but at
Amazon right now it's $4.52.

01:05:49.446 --> 01:05:52.025
I can't get them
that cheaply so,

01:05:52.026 --> 01:05:53.640
and you can only order four.

01:05:53.641 --> 01:05:55.355
So like all of my friends

01:05:55.356 --> 01:05:58.780
order books and bring
them to my house at $4.52,

01:05:58.781 --> 01:06:00.270
so yeah st 4.52 at Amazon.

01:06:00.271 --> 01:06:01.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah I can confirm
the pay back is,

01:06:01.591 --> 01:06:04.800
I was about to buy two copies
because my question is,

01:06:04.801 --> 01:06:07.210
as Elementary School kids,

01:06:07.211 --> 01:06:09.850
and we have a science project
that was due today

01:06:09.851 --> 01:06:12.320
and we have one parent me who

01:06:12.321 --> 01:06:14.470
just kind of steps back
and watches the kid do

01:06:14.471 --> 01:06:16.905
things and then
my wife just jumps in

01:06:16.906 --> 01:06:19.825
and starts doing things but

01:06:19.826 --> 01:06:22.980
then I'm also the one at
the end that exercises praises,

01:06:22.981 --> 01:06:25.305
oh that's amazing, oh this
is going to be stellar,

01:06:25.306 --> 01:06:28.260
you're so amazing, everything.

01:06:28.380 --> 01:06:31.385
Instead of like what you
need to do with your kid,

01:06:31.386 --> 01:06:33.300
what advice do you have for
parents with one another?

01:06:33.301 --> 01:06:37.010
&gt;&gt; Okay, so, what this
really comes down to is

01:06:37.011 --> 01:06:38.680
like there are
these big takeaways and on

01:06:38.681 --> 01:06:41.120
the bookmark my son gave you
there are some takeaways.

01:06:41.121 --> 01:06:42.580
One of the biggest
takeaways like

01:06:42.581 --> 01:06:44.770
the fastest way to switch
your thinking right

01:06:44.771 --> 01:06:46.290
now is to be thinking

01:06:46.291 --> 01:06:48.515
long-term about what
you want for your kids.

01:06:48.516 --> 01:06:51.250
So do you want
this science project

01:06:51.251 --> 01:06:53.815
right here to be perfect

01:06:53.816 --> 01:06:56.860
or do you want your kid
next year when the science

01:06:56.861 --> 01:07:00.095
fair rolls around to not be
doing it the night before.

01:07:00.096 --> 01:07:04.140
So real-life story Finnegan,
another authorized story.

01:07:04.141 --> 01:07:06.220
Finnegan had
a science fair project

01:07:06.221 --> 01:07:08.920
that he so half-asked,

01:07:08.921 --> 01:07:10.280
it wasn't even half-asked,

01:07:10.281 --> 01:07:13.370
it was like a sliver
asked and it was,

01:07:13.371 --> 01:07:15.520
it came down to
a piece of trifold with

01:07:15.521 --> 01:07:20.600
some sharpie big stars and
filling up space over here,

01:07:20.601 --> 01:07:22.465
look over here,
that kind of thing.

01:07:22.466 --> 01:07:24.560
And then he had to
go to the science

01:07:24.561 --> 01:07:27.470
fair and sit there and
defend his project for

01:07:27.471 --> 01:07:30.420
two hours next to

01:07:30.421 --> 01:07:31.550
the girl who has the like

01:07:31.551 --> 01:07:33.680
life-sized hologram of the uni-,

01:07:33.681 --> 01:07:35.785
it's a scaled hologram
of the universe that she

01:07:35.786 --> 01:07:39.345
totally did herself
of course not.

01:07:39.346 --> 01:07:43.435
But that was an incredibly
important lesson.

01:07:43.436 --> 01:07:45.330
Having to sit there in
front of something you

01:07:45.331 --> 01:07:47.825
created or didn't
create and defend it,

01:07:47.826 --> 01:07:49.910
is a really tough thing
to do but it's also

01:07:49.911 --> 01:07:52.150
an incredible
learning experience

01:07:52.151 --> 01:07:54.200
because the next time he had to

01:07:54.201 --> 01:07:55.440
do a science project

01:07:55.441 --> 01:07:57.220
and do you know which one
I'm talking about sweetie?

01:07:57.221 --> 01:07:59.025
No, he's listening
to headphones.

01:07:59.026 --> 01:08:01.140
Do you know which science
project I'm talking about that

01:08:01.141 --> 01:08:03.425
was the killer
one the grays one?

01:08:03.426 --> 01:08:04.200
&gt;&gt; That one.

01:08:04.201 --> 01:08:05.100
&gt;&gt; The one with the alien?

01:08:05.101 --> 01:08:05.551
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:08:05.551 --> 01:08:07.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was really, really
good. So I didn't even get

01:08:07.441 --> 01:08:09.360
to see that project
until it was ready to

01:08:09.361 --> 01:08:13.150
go to the science
fair and it was

01:08:13.151 --> 01:08:15.650
so far beyond what
I thought my kid was

01:08:15.651 --> 01:08:18.310
capable of simply
because he's like "Well,

01:08:18.311 --> 01:08:20.830
I'm not doing what I did
last time and sitting

01:08:20.831 --> 01:08:24.472
there and being humiliated
because that wasn't fun."

01:08:24.473 --> 01:08:26.335
No, it wasn't fun, but it was

01:08:26.336 --> 01:08:27.825
incredibly good
learning experience.

01:08:27.826 --> 01:08:31.025
So, thinking long-term
instead of short-term,

01:08:31.026 --> 01:08:33.150
my kid can do X today,

01:08:33.151 --> 01:08:34.860
but where do I want them to be

01:08:34.861 --> 01:08:36.790
in six months, in
a year, whatever?

01:08:36.791 --> 01:08:38.165
You can even think in terms of,

01:08:38.166 --> 01:08:40.345
my kids are going to go off
to college when they're 18,

01:08:40.346 --> 01:08:42.095
what do I need them
to be able to do?

01:08:42.096 --> 01:08:44.275
Make a checklist. There are
great checklists out there.

01:08:44.276 --> 01:08:45.585
My friend, Julie Lythcott-Haims,

01:08:45.586 --> 01:08:46.665
has a book called
"How to Raise an

01:08:46.666 --> 01:08:47.895
Adult". She's got checklist.

01:08:47.896 --> 01:08:49.340
I have checklists in the book,

01:08:49.341 --> 01:08:51.275
age appropriate
checklist of what kids

01:08:51.276 --> 01:08:53.245
are actually capable of doing,

01:08:53.246 --> 01:08:54.605
that we don't ever ask them to

01:08:54.606 --> 01:08:56.210
do because we're like, "Well,

01:08:56.211 --> 01:08:58.180
of course, they can't do that,

01:08:58.181 --> 01:09:00.645
that requires too
much whatever."

01:09:00.646 --> 01:09:03.240
Long-term instead
of short-term is

01:09:03.241 --> 01:09:06.045
the most important thing and
process instead of product.

01:09:06.046 --> 01:09:08.515
I mean, if you come down
to the fact that the

01:09:08.516 --> 01:09:11.210
doing for all good
projects at school,

01:09:11.211 --> 01:09:13.385
really the doing of
the project should always be

01:09:13.386 --> 01:09:16.195
far more important than
whatever the end product is,

01:09:16.196 --> 01:09:17.895
it just so happens that kids

01:09:17.896 --> 01:09:19.335
do like creating an end product.

01:09:19.336 --> 01:09:20.375
So that's a great way to

01:09:20.376 --> 01:09:22.025
sort of show something
off at the end.

01:09:22.026 --> 01:09:23.975
Focus on process over product.

01:09:23.976 --> 01:09:27.505
Focus on the parts that are
difficult for them saying,

01:09:27.506 --> 01:09:29.605
"I'm really proud of you
for sticking with that

01:09:29.606 --> 01:09:31.970
when I saw how hard
that was for you.

01:09:31.971 --> 01:09:33.235
Long division maybe a

01:09:33.236 --> 01:09:34.765
something that's
really hard for you.

01:09:34.766 --> 01:09:36.415
And you know what,
I watched you do

01:09:36.416 --> 01:09:37.585
your homework because you

01:09:37.586 --> 01:09:38.895
thought I was just
cooking over here,

01:09:38.896 --> 01:09:40.555
but I was peaking
every once in a while.

01:09:40.556 --> 01:09:42.985
And I saw that number
three just gave you fits.

01:09:42.986 --> 01:09:44.365
But you know what, you went

01:09:44.366 --> 01:09:45.825
on to four and you
came back to three,

01:09:45.826 --> 01:09:47.450
and I'm really proud
of you for that."

01:09:47.451 --> 01:09:49.115
That's praising for effort,

01:09:49.116 --> 01:09:51.505
that's constructive
praise for effort.

01:09:51.506 --> 01:09:53.825
But that long-term
over short-term man,

01:09:53.826 --> 01:09:56.025
that not only works for
our kids. It works for us.

01:09:56.026 --> 01:09:57.605
It works for all kinds
of stuff because we tend

01:09:57.606 --> 01:09:59.565
to parent in emergency mode.

01:09:59.566 --> 01:10:01.565
This homework has to be perfect.

01:10:01.566 --> 01:10:03.815
The kid doesn't
have lunch today.

01:10:03.816 --> 01:10:05.185
Will she survive?

01:10:05.186 --> 01:10:07.060
Yes, she will survive.

01:10:07.061 --> 01:10:09.810
The fastest way
I tell schools if

01:10:09.811 --> 01:10:12.405
they say, "This is
all well and good."

01:10:12.406 --> 01:10:13.880
How do we send a message really,

01:10:13.881 --> 01:10:15.845
really quickly to
the entire community that we

01:10:15.846 --> 01:10:18.005
care more about the process
than the product,

01:10:18.006 --> 01:10:20.295
that we care more about
the whole child than the grades?

01:10:20.296 --> 01:10:22.395
I say, one of the great things
that schools have been

01:10:22.396 --> 01:10:25.145
doing increasingly around
the country is making a rule,

01:10:25.146 --> 01:10:26.725
that there's no
dropping forgotten

01:10:26.726 --> 01:10:29.070
stuff off after first bell.

01:10:29.071 --> 01:10:32.375
Not only is that sending

01:10:32.376 --> 01:10:34.945
the message that what you
really care about is that

01:10:34.946 --> 01:10:37.155
the kid learns to manage
their time and learns how to use

01:10:37.156 --> 01:10:38.855
that plan book and can

01:10:38.856 --> 01:10:41.415
cope with the consequences
of their actions,

01:10:41.416 --> 01:10:43.855
but it's also fair if
you think about who can

01:10:43.856 --> 01:10:46.365
afford to bring
stuff to school late,

01:10:46.366 --> 01:10:47.545
it's certainly not people who do

01:10:47.546 --> 01:10:49.665
shift work or rely on
public transportation.

01:10:49.666 --> 01:10:51.870
So, from an equity
equality perspective,

01:10:51.871 --> 01:10:53.375
it tends to be
a really great thing

01:10:53.376 --> 01:10:54.995
for the school district.

01:10:54.996 --> 01:10:57.145
So that's my
recommendation to schools,

01:10:57.146 --> 01:11:00.565
is that rule works
wonders actually.

01:11:00.566 --> 01:11:04.125
It's stepping away.

01:11:04.126 --> 01:11:06.295
It's also what works when
other parents are going nuts,

01:11:06.296 --> 01:11:07.885
and they're like,
"Can we do cello?

01:11:07.886 --> 01:11:08.995
And we have all this tutoring,

01:11:08.996 --> 01:11:10.435
and he's on the
traveling soccer league."

01:11:10.436 --> 01:11:12.085
And you start to hyperventilate.

01:11:12.086 --> 01:11:13.935
I wrote an article for
the Atlantic called "Why

01:11:13.936 --> 01:11:16.015
Back-to-School Night Made
Me Feel Like a Bad Parent".

01:11:16.016 --> 01:11:17.825
Step away, we are

01:11:17.826 --> 01:11:19.935
each other's worst enemy
when it comes to that stuff.

01:11:19.936 --> 01:11:22.045
So, step away. And
if you have to go,

01:11:22.046 --> 01:11:23.990
I totally, totally get it.

01:11:23.991 --> 01:11:26.645
Bibliography is at
jessicalahey.com.

01:11:26.646 --> 01:11:29.205
If you sign up on my website,

01:11:29.206 --> 01:11:31.285
there's a newsletter with
all the links to everything,

01:11:31.286 --> 01:11:33.265
plus there's a new

01:11:33.266 --> 01:11:36.165
book discussion group guide
that has questions

01:11:36.166 --> 01:11:37.950
for each chapter
and stuff like that

01:11:37.951 --> 01:11:41.190
and the bibliography and
all that fun stuff. Okay, sorry.

01:11:41.191 --> 01:11:43.685
&gt;&gt; You talked about
expectations and consequences

01:11:43.686 --> 01:11:46.315
and setting realistic things
for your children.

01:11:46.316 --> 01:11:48.295
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; What kind of consequences?

01:11:48.296 --> 01:11:50.730
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Is that kind of an extrinsic?

01:11:50.731 --> 01:11:53.095
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So that's tough.

01:11:53.096 --> 01:11:56.155
So, often consequences
tend to be extrinsic,

01:11:56.156 --> 01:11:57.395
like punishment or I'm

01:11:57.396 --> 01:11:59.140
going to take that thing
away from you.

01:11:59.141 --> 01:12:01.485
Especially the take
that thing away from you,

01:12:01.486 --> 01:12:02.505
we tend to say
we're going to take

01:12:02.506 --> 01:12:03.895
those electronics away from you,

01:12:03.896 --> 01:12:05.345
because in our adult brain,

01:12:05.346 --> 01:12:06.710
that makes so much sense

01:12:06.711 --> 01:12:08.605
because we're killing
two birds with one stone.

01:12:08.606 --> 01:12:10.310
Not only will they be bummed out

01:12:10.311 --> 01:12:12.295
and not want to lose
their electronics,

01:12:12.296 --> 01:12:13.825
but if they lose
their electronics,

01:12:13.826 --> 01:12:15.365
they will have more time to work

01:12:15.366 --> 01:12:17.485
on their homework.
It's so brilliant.

01:12:17.486 --> 01:12:19.640
Except for the fact
that for kids

01:12:19.641 --> 01:12:21.565
whose frontal lobes aren't

01:12:21.566 --> 01:12:23.350
fully cooked, it makes no sense.

01:12:23.351 --> 01:12:27.135
It's like saying, "If you
can't build this treehouse,

01:12:27.136 --> 01:12:28.495
I'm going to send you to Mars."

01:12:28.496 --> 01:12:32.505
Because electronics, and
if you think about it,

01:12:32.506 --> 01:12:34.515
normally what we're
saying is, "So

01:12:34.516 --> 01:12:36.985
this is September or
April, August, September.

01:12:36.986 --> 01:12:40.755
If in December your grade falls,

01:12:40.756 --> 01:12:42.805
we're going to take
away your electronics."

01:12:42.806 --> 01:12:44.005
And you have a kid, let's say,

01:12:44.006 --> 01:12:45.355
middle school kid who's focused

01:12:45.356 --> 01:12:46.645
on this homework assignment,

01:12:46.646 --> 01:12:47.865
should I really put in my

01:12:47.866 --> 01:12:49.645
all on this homework assignment?

01:12:49.646 --> 01:12:53.815
They are very bad at saying
this homework assignment is

01:12:53.816 --> 01:12:55.825
related to this thing
over here in

01:12:55.826 --> 01:12:58.745
December that's totally
unrelated to school,

01:12:58.746 --> 01:13:01.605
my electronics,
those two things don't equate,

01:13:01.606 --> 01:13:05.165
so the closer you can
make consequences to

01:13:05.166 --> 01:13:07.755
the actual consequences that

01:13:07.756 --> 01:13:10.215
happen when a kid
doesn't do something.

01:13:10.216 --> 01:13:11.385
So, for example, with homework,

01:13:11.386 --> 01:13:13.980
the classic in our house
anyway would be,

01:13:13.981 --> 01:13:17.075
if you don't get
your homework in to

01:13:17.076 --> 01:13:20.645
the teacher and it
becomes a thing,

01:13:20.646 --> 01:13:22.985
like it once always happens,

01:13:22.986 --> 01:13:24.740
that's a process of learning,

01:13:24.741 --> 01:13:30.975
then you will lead the
parent-teacher-kid conference,

01:13:30.976 --> 01:13:34.295
where we talk about how
we can best help you,

01:13:34.296 --> 01:13:36.070
where we can talk
about strategy.

01:13:36.071 --> 01:13:37.355
We're not going to lead that,

01:13:37.356 --> 01:13:38.395
you are, because you're the

01:13:38.396 --> 01:13:39.545
one who's forgetting
your homework.

01:13:39.546 --> 01:13:40.585
So you have to set that meeting

01:13:40.586 --> 01:13:41.815
up and you have
to make it happen.

01:13:41.816 --> 01:13:43.355
You can work behind

01:13:43.356 --> 01:13:45.255
the scenes to kind
of pull some strings.

01:13:45.256 --> 01:13:47.495
So you can email
the teacher and say,

01:13:47.496 --> 01:13:49.075
"My kid's going
to come up to you

01:13:49.076 --> 01:13:50.870
today and ask you to
make an appointment.

01:13:50.871 --> 01:13:52.065
And if that doesn't happen,

01:13:52.066 --> 01:13:54.470
could you let me know
or could you sort of

01:13:54.471 --> 01:13:56.230
engineer a moment
where it might be

01:13:56.231 --> 01:13:57.820
easier for the kid to ask that?"

01:13:57.821 --> 01:13:59.775
I do that as a teacher
all the time.

01:13:59.776 --> 01:14:01.195
Parents will get in
touch with me and say,

01:14:01.196 --> 01:14:02.425
"She really needs to talk to

01:14:02.426 --> 01:14:03.950
you about something
she's upset about.

01:14:03.951 --> 01:14:05.695
And I have a feeling
she's not going to.

01:14:05.696 --> 01:14:08.410
So, could you kind of make
an opportune moment for that?

01:14:08.411 --> 01:14:10.060
That's really helpful for us."

01:14:10.061 --> 01:14:11.125
By the way, those of you who

01:14:11.126 --> 01:14:12.705
help your kids with homework,

01:14:12.706 --> 01:14:17.025
homework is information for us.

01:14:17.026 --> 01:14:19.975
So, a classic in Math class,

01:14:19.976 --> 01:14:21.625
a friend of mine talks
about this all the time,

01:14:21.626 --> 01:14:23.355
that she'll have in Algebra one,

01:14:23.356 --> 01:14:24.545
she'll get homework back that

01:14:24.546 --> 01:14:26.315
uses trigonometry to
figure something out,

01:14:26.316 --> 01:14:27.405
and she'll ask
the kids, "So, how

01:14:27.406 --> 01:14:28.615
did you arrive at this answer?"

01:14:28.616 --> 01:14:31.635
And the kids are
like, "I don't know,

01:14:31.636 --> 01:14:33.935
ask my dad. I don't know."

01:14:33.936 --> 01:14:34.955
And it's pretty clear

01:14:34.956 --> 01:14:37.325
that the kid hasn't
been doing the work,

01:14:37.326 --> 01:14:40.900
and whatever, except
for the fact that

01:14:40.901 --> 01:14:45.200
now the teacher can't tell
what the kid can and can't do.

01:14:45.201 --> 01:14:49.480
So, the more opportunities
for information, the better.

01:14:49.481 --> 01:14:51.545
So, consequences really should,

01:14:51.546 --> 01:14:52.885
as best you can,

01:14:52.886 --> 01:14:57.555
make them flow from whatever
it was the kid didn't do.

01:14:57.556 --> 01:14:59.250
A friend of mine, at
the end of the book,

01:14:59.251 --> 01:15:01.005
inevitably someone
asked the question of,

01:15:01.006 --> 01:15:02.355
"Well, what about real failure

01:15:02.356 --> 01:15:05.080
like failing out
of school failure?"

01:15:05.081 --> 01:15:07.345
There's a story at the end
of the book about that.

01:15:07.346 --> 01:15:10.305
Someone whose son was failing
out of school because he

01:15:10.306 --> 01:15:11.795
refused to sort of take

01:15:11.796 --> 01:15:14.740
command of the
situation and work.

01:15:14.741 --> 01:15:17.225
She and I talked about getting

01:15:17.226 --> 01:15:19.545
a probationary semester
for her son.

01:15:19.546 --> 01:15:21.235
She negotiated this probationary

01:15:21.236 --> 01:15:22.980
semester before
they kicked him out,

01:15:22.981 --> 01:15:25.060
and we got really lucky there.

01:15:25.061 --> 01:15:26.825
But then, she had
to say to her son,

01:15:26.826 --> 01:15:28.735
"You have this
probationary semester.

01:15:28.736 --> 01:15:30.485
So you're going to

01:15:30.486 --> 01:15:33.235
go for a couple days
to this other school,

01:15:33.236 --> 01:15:34.445
which is where
you'll have to go to

01:15:34.446 --> 01:15:36.470
school if you flunk
out of this school."

01:15:36.471 --> 01:15:39.015
And it was not
a great choice for him.

01:15:39.016 --> 01:15:41.845
It was not a really great school
for him to go to.

01:15:41.846 --> 01:15:44.415
And he attended
that other school for the day.

01:15:44.416 --> 01:15:45.665
And he came home,
and he's like, "Okay,

01:15:45.666 --> 01:15:47.045
I'm in, what do I have to do?"

01:15:47.046 --> 01:15:49.105
And she said, "Well, you
should also know that I'm not

01:15:49.106 --> 01:15:51.175
going to be completing
stuff for you anymore.

01:15:51.176 --> 01:15:52.935
This is you, this
is your education,

01:15:52.936 --> 01:15:56.020
this is your decision,
we need buy-in from you."

01:15:56.021 --> 01:15:58.415
He still talks
about that moment,

01:15:58.416 --> 01:16:00.505
the kid I kept in touch
with the parent and the kid,

01:16:00.506 --> 01:16:02.555
as being the turning
point in his life.

01:16:02.556 --> 01:16:04.530
And I get to report this

01:16:04.531 --> 01:16:06.595
because she just
found out recently,

01:16:06.596 --> 01:16:10.055
he's going to college
now on full scholarship.

01:16:10.056 --> 01:16:12.675
He not only took control
of his education,

01:16:12.676 --> 01:16:13.905
completely turned it around.

01:16:13.906 --> 01:16:17.115
And by the way, when a kid
turns around education,

01:16:17.116 --> 01:16:18.535
that's when we get to write

01:16:18.536 --> 01:16:20.930
the really good letters
of recommendation.

01:16:20.931 --> 01:16:23.465
The kids who have just been
sort of coasting or kids who

01:16:23.466 --> 01:16:26.375
just sort of had been
perfect all along,

01:16:26.376 --> 01:16:30.180
those letters are whatever,
they're fun, fine.

01:16:30.181 --> 01:16:32.385
But the letters of kids
who really have sort

01:16:32.386 --> 01:16:35.340
of taken that
opportunity to change,

01:16:35.341 --> 01:16:37.685
who get inspired and
decide to change things,

01:16:37.686 --> 01:16:39.735
those are the letters that
get them into college.

01:16:39.736 --> 01:16:41.575
And that's what
happened with this kid.

01:16:41.576 --> 01:16:43.355
He had more scholarships to

01:16:43.356 --> 01:16:45.625
choose from than he
knew what to do with.

01:16:45.626 --> 01:16:46.985
And so, he's going
to college for

01:16:46.986 --> 01:16:49.765
free, his dream college.

01:16:49.766 --> 01:16:53.155
And really because
he nearly failed out,

01:16:53.156 --> 01:16:55.785
so he has a story to tell
about his transcript.

01:16:55.786 --> 01:16:57.180
He was able to say, "Look,

01:16:57.181 --> 01:16:58.405
you look at my transcript,

01:16:58.406 --> 01:17:00.835
and you think I really was
falling apart freshman year,

01:17:00.836 --> 01:17:02.105
let me tell you that story.

01:17:02.106 --> 01:17:03.805
And let me tell you where I am

01:17:03.806 --> 01:17:06.000
now as a human being
because of that story."

01:17:06.001 --> 01:17:08.545
He had to suffer
the real consequences.

01:17:08.546 --> 01:17:11.825
And if he had failed
out of school,

01:17:11.826 --> 01:17:13.220
I know for a fact
his mother would

01:17:13.221 --> 01:17:14.955
have taken him and put
him in that other school,

01:17:14.956 --> 01:17:16.950
and he would have had
to sink or swim there.

01:17:16.951 --> 01:17:19.630
It ended up being
a fantastic opportunity

01:17:19.631 --> 01:17:22.165
for him and maybe
even opportunity for

01:17:22.166 --> 01:17:23.345
him to realize that wasn't what

01:17:23.346 --> 01:17:24.635
he wanted and find
out if there would

01:17:24.636 --> 01:17:25.695
be a way that he could work

01:17:25.696 --> 01:17:27.155
his way back into
the other school.

01:17:27.156 --> 01:17:29.287
That's always a possibility too.

01:17:29.288 --> 01:17:32.305
Yes. Behind you.

01:17:32.306 --> 01:17:34.780
Yeah, yeah, sorry.

01:17:34.781 --> 01:17:37.290
I have a room by
the way till 1:30

01:17:37.291 --> 01:17:39.540
but I'm happy to stick
around and answer questions,

01:17:39.541 --> 01:17:41.285
as long as I'm not
going anywhere.

01:17:41.286 --> 01:17:44.250
&gt;&gt; Okay. I totally understand as

01:17:44.251 --> 01:17:48.955
parents not wanting to be
controlled and corrected.

01:17:48.956 --> 01:17:52.690
I happened to have my oldest
going to sixth grader.

01:17:52.691 --> 01:17:55.590
And he just really

01:17:55.591 --> 01:17:57.910
doesn't want to work hard
when it comes to school work.

01:17:57.911 --> 01:17:58.401
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:17:58.401 --> 01:18:00.355
&gt;&gt; He's very bright.

01:18:00.356 --> 01:18:05.275
If that means he cannot
work hard on this assignment

01:18:05.276 --> 01:18:07.370
or not study much
for this test and

01:18:07.371 --> 01:18:09.570
he gets a B, he
is fine with that.

01:18:09.571 --> 01:18:10.001
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:18:10.001 --> 01:18:11.290
&gt;&gt; I'm not fine with that.

01:18:11.291 --> 01:18:11.491
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:18:11.491 --> 01:18:14.075
&gt;&gt; Because I know he put
zero effort into that B, right?

01:18:14.076 --> 01:18:14.411
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:18:14.411 --> 01:18:16.990
&gt;&gt; And this is
a constant argument with us,

01:18:16.991 --> 01:18:18.520
and it's also, so what
do you do with that kid.

01:18:18.521 --> 01:18:20.140
&gt;&gt; Okay, so number
one if you want

01:18:20.141 --> 01:18:22.935
a really thorough 15
minute long answer,

01:18:22.936 --> 01:18:24.100
it's at the gift of

01:18:24.101 --> 01:18:26.830
failure facts to how to
motivate kids who coast.

01:18:26.831 --> 01:18:27.370
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:18:27.371 --> 01:18:29.020
&gt;&gt; What you also
need to know from

01:18:29.021 --> 01:18:30.970
a developmental
perspective is this.

01:18:30.971 --> 01:18:32.590
Until you get to middle school

01:18:32.591 --> 01:18:33.960
like sixth grade, seventh grade,

01:18:33.961 --> 01:18:36.260
eighth grade, really
the end of fifth grade,

01:18:36.261 --> 01:18:38.525
kids are really
focused internally.

01:18:38.526 --> 01:18:39.745
And they're really focused

01:18:39.746 --> 01:18:41.450
on getting your love
and approval.

01:18:41.451 --> 01:18:43.070
They're really
focused on how you're

01:18:43.071 --> 01:18:45.460
reacting to what they're doing.

01:18:45.461 --> 01:18:47.820
Fifth grade, sixth grade,
seventh grade,

01:18:47.821 --> 01:18:50.150
suddenly the world opens up.

01:18:50.151 --> 01:18:53.880
The social life opens up and
hormones start to kick in,

01:18:53.881 --> 01:18:55.940
and oh my gosh people
are looking at me,

01:18:55.941 --> 01:18:57.540
and oh my gosh for,

01:18:57.541 --> 01:18:59.080
the big joke in middle school

01:18:59.081 --> 01:19:00.200
is that all middle schoolers

01:19:00.201 --> 01:19:02.820
believed that everyone is
looking at them all the time,

01:19:02.821 --> 01:19:05.590
which means that they have
to be focused on themselves,

01:19:05.591 --> 01:19:07.430
and everyone is so
focused on themselves,

01:19:07.431 --> 01:19:09.100
they are not actually
looking at each other.

01:19:09.101 --> 01:19:12.200
So, they're now more
outwardly focused.

01:19:12.201 --> 01:19:13.770
And friendships tend to become

01:19:13.771 --> 01:19:15.945
more outwardly focused,
and so they become,

01:19:15.946 --> 01:19:18.955
that takes front seat
for a little while and

01:19:18.956 --> 01:19:21.140
understanding that
is going to go

01:19:21.141 --> 01:19:24.135
a long way towards helping
you understand the motivation.

01:19:24.136 --> 01:19:27.130
At least understanding why
your kid maybe pulling back

01:19:27.131 --> 01:19:28.440
from the school stuff

01:19:28.441 --> 01:19:31.330
which maybe the year before
he was all over that.

01:19:31.331 --> 01:19:34.540
So, understanding
motivation is really,

01:19:34.541 --> 01:19:36.170
really important because
that helps you understand

01:19:36.171 --> 01:19:37.920
where your fulcrum point is.

01:19:37.921 --> 01:19:41.050
Where those levers
can get installed,

01:19:41.051 --> 01:19:43.160
and knowing what their goals
are is going to be

01:19:43.161 --> 01:19:45.190
really important. You
can talk to your kid.

01:19:45.191 --> 01:19:46.990
If you happen to know
that your kid wants

01:19:46.991 --> 01:19:49.900
to get into a certain
Math class the next year,

01:19:49.901 --> 01:19:51.840
or your kid wants to go
to a certain college,

01:19:51.841 --> 01:19:54.310
or your kid wants to do
a certain thing for work,

01:19:54.311 --> 01:19:56.770
one of the things
like kid says I really

01:19:56.771 --> 01:19:59.650
want to be an architect,
I don't know whatever.

01:19:59.651 --> 01:20:02.510
You can talk to them
about the fact that well,

01:20:02.511 --> 01:20:04.385
how do you plan to get there?

01:20:04.386 --> 01:20:06.310
One of the things I
do a lot as an adviser

01:20:06.311 --> 01:20:08.160
to kids is not

01:20:08.161 --> 01:20:09.840
just talk about what were
their long-term goals are,

01:20:09.841 --> 01:20:11.715
because well that's
all well and good.

01:20:11.716 --> 01:20:13.960
Talking to them about the goals,

01:20:13.961 --> 01:20:15.925
the short-term achievable
goals they have

01:20:15.926 --> 01:20:18.210
to get through to get to
those long-term goals,

01:20:18.211 --> 01:20:20.240
that's called self-directed
executive function,

01:20:20.241 --> 01:20:23.130
when they can come up with
those intermediary steps

01:20:23.131 --> 01:20:25.140
to get to the big
goal out there.

01:20:25.141 --> 01:20:27.280
It can be less
threatening to talk about

01:20:27.281 --> 01:20:29.905
these short-term goals than
that big one out there,

01:20:29.906 --> 01:20:32.420
and it's also more about
process than product.

01:20:32.421 --> 01:20:35.460
So if you happen to know
that your kid really wants to

01:20:35.461 --> 01:20:36.970
play soccer but you have to have

01:20:36.971 --> 01:20:38.750
a C or better to play soccer,

01:20:38.751 --> 01:20:40.290
then there's your fulcrum point.

01:20:40.291 --> 01:20:41.950
If you happen to know
that your kid wants to

01:20:41.951 --> 01:20:43.690
go to this high school
as opposed to that one,

01:20:43.691 --> 01:20:46.090
but this high school is a
little bit harder to get into,

01:20:46.091 --> 01:20:47.600
then you can know well,

01:20:47.601 --> 01:20:48.700
that's your fulcrum point.

01:20:48.701 --> 01:20:50.360
If you really want to
be over there let's talk

01:20:50.361 --> 01:20:52.755
about your strategies for
how to make that happen.

01:20:52.756 --> 01:20:55.680
For kids who are coasting,

01:20:55.681 --> 01:21:00.670
honestly, the reaction they're
giving out of you for not,

01:21:00.671 --> 01:21:03.835
sometimes for not putting
in that extra little bit,

01:21:03.836 --> 01:21:06.320
it can also be, remember
when I talked about kids not

01:21:06.321 --> 01:21:08.760
wanting to fail and that's
really scary for them.

01:21:08.761 --> 01:21:10.190
If you're telling
them constantly,

01:21:10.191 --> 01:21:12.470
look, we know you're not
working up to your potential,

01:21:12.471 --> 01:21:13.900
we know you're
smarter than this,

01:21:13.901 --> 01:21:15.500
in the back of the head
one of the things

01:21:15.501 --> 01:21:18.000
that kids can hear is,
but what if I'm not.

01:21:18.001 --> 01:21:21.525
And so going that extra step
can be really scary

01:21:21.526 --> 01:21:24.210
because what if I'm

01:21:24.211 --> 01:21:26.410
not as smart as
other people think I am?

01:21:26.411 --> 01:21:27.540
What if my friends find out?

01:21:27.541 --> 01:21:29.600
What if my teachers find out?

01:21:29.601 --> 01:21:33.010
So focusing more on the process
rather than that end

01:21:33.011 --> 01:21:36.285
product can sort of
help them get over that.

01:21:36.286 --> 01:21:38.540
The other thing that can help,

01:21:38.541 --> 01:21:40.780
when I go to talk to kids,

01:21:40.781 --> 01:21:42.520
you can- I talk to them

01:21:42.521 --> 01:21:44.880
about I look at
the audience and I say,

01:21:44.881 --> 01:21:48.110
if you're coasting and
you know who you are

01:21:48.111 --> 01:21:51.970
you are not becoming smarter
as fast as your classmates,

01:21:51.971 --> 01:21:53.965
because what we know
about the brain is,

01:21:53.966 --> 01:21:56.215
if you read Carol
Dweck's "Mindset",

01:21:56.216 --> 01:21:57.830
what we know is
that the harder we

01:21:57.831 --> 01:21:59.815
push ourselves and this
goes for adults too.

01:21:59.816 --> 01:22:01.320
The more we push
ourselves outside of

01:22:01.321 --> 01:22:03.315
our comfort zone like
if I were to take,

01:22:03.316 --> 01:22:05.490
I don't know ballroom
dancing which would

01:22:05.491 --> 01:22:08.055
be a nightmare scenario for me,

01:22:08.056 --> 01:22:09.850
because I just, I'm clumsy.

01:22:09.851 --> 01:22:12.265
But I would be making
connections in my brain.

01:22:12.266 --> 01:22:14.855
I would become less clumsy.

01:22:14.856 --> 01:22:16.550
If I am not great at

01:22:16.551 --> 01:22:18.820
Math but I push
myself harder in Math,

01:22:18.821 --> 01:22:20.450
I'm learning more because I'm

01:22:20.451 --> 01:22:21.990
making more connections
in our brain,

01:22:21.991 --> 01:22:25.390
that sort of the backwards
when we tell older people

01:22:25.391 --> 01:22:27.210
to do crossword puzzles so that

01:22:27.211 --> 01:22:29.355
they don't lose
those connections with kids,

01:22:29.356 --> 01:22:30.730
the more connections
they make the

01:22:30.731 --> 01:22:32.060
smarter they will become,

01:22:32.061 --> 01:22:33.555
because that's how
our brains work.

01:22:33.556 --> 01:22:36.810
We're not born with
an IQ number and God

01:22:36.811 --> 01:22:39.300
forbid if you have

01:22:39.301 --> 01:22:41.090
to get your kid an IQ test
for some reason,

01:22:41.091 --> 01:22:42.480
and I actually post
it that there's

01:22:42.481 --> 01:22:44.585
no really good reason
to do that,

01:22:44.586 --> 01:22:47.550
the IQ test was never
intended to be a measure

01:22:47.551 --> 01:22:50.410
of a numerical measure
of intelligence.

01:22:50.411 --> 01:22:51.610
There's a fantastic book by

01:22:51.611 --> 01:22:54.350
Scott Barry Kaufman
called "Ungifted ",

01:22:54.351 --> 01:22:57.030
where he talks about
the history of the IQ test.

01:22:57.031 --> 01:22:59.735
He tells this story
through the lens of

01:22:59.736 --> 01:23:02.990
a person who was
Special Ed and now has PhD,

01:23:02.991 --> 01:23:09.960
and he works basically on
the neuroscience of creativity,

01:23:09.961 --> 01:23:11.540
and the psychology
of creativity,

01:23:11.541 --> 01:23:12.790
and what intelligence and

01:23:12.791 --> 01:23:14.855
creativity have to
do with one another.

01:23:14.856 --> 01:23:18.260
For example, someone with

01:23:18.261 --> 01:23:21.380
dyslexia may score
lower on an IQ test,

01:23:21.381 --> 01:23:23.060
but how many people in

01:23:23.061 --> 01:23:25.355
this room know there's
an upside to dyslexia?

01:23:25.356 --> 01:23:28.710
That there are things people
with dyslexia can do that

01:23:28.711 --> 01:23:31.865
people without dyslexia can't
or at least not as well?

01:23:31.866 --> 01:23:34.510
Yeah. Some of the most
amazing astronomers

01:23:34.511 --> 01:23:36.930
we've had in the history
of humankind have been

01:23:36.931 --> 01:23:38.470
dyslexic because they have

01:23:38.471 --> 01:23:41.365
the ability to see
sort of relationships

01:23:41.366 --> 01:23:43.660
between things and
distance and time in

01:23:43.661 --> 01:23:46.165
a way that people who
are non-dyslexic don't.

01:23:46.166 --> 01:23:47.260
There's a great book called,

01:23:47.261 --> 01:23:48.410
"The Upside of Dyslexia."

01:23:48.411 --> 01:23:51.045
By the way that's
all about that.

01:23:51.046 --> 01:23:54.620
Most IQ tests don't-
Most intelligence tests

01:23:54.621 --> 01:23:55.950
don't take things like

01:23:55.951 --> 01:23:57.750
creativity into account or

01:23:57.751 --> 01:24:00.545
your individual quirks
about how you think.

01:24:00.546 --> 01:24:02.040
And so Scott Barry Kaufman is

01:24:02.041 --> 01:24:04.080
currently working to
try to come up with

01:24:04.081 --> 01:24:06.200
a measure of intelligence
that encompasses things

01:24:06.201 --> 01:24:09.060
like being a people person,

01:24:09.061 --> 01:24:11.350
being creative person,
thinking in a way

01:24:11.351 --> 01:24:14.380
that's different from
your peers think.

01:24:14.381 --> 01:24:16.720
So putting as much focus
on things like that,

01:24:16.721 --> 01:24:18.600
and understanding where
this is coming from,

01:24:18.601 --> 01:24:21.550
and also understanding
that your kid

01:24:21.551 --> 01:24:26.000
really truly believes because
we tend to talk good game.

01:24:26.001 --> 01:24:27.640
Does your kid really truly

01:24:27.641 --> 01:24:28.880
believe that what you care about

01:24:28.881 --> 01:24:31.450
is learning or is it
just a lot of this?

01:24:31.451 --> 01:24:34.420
Because if they see
us freaking out over

01:24:34.421 --> 01:24:35.890
a low grade or going all

01:24:35.891 --> 01:24:37.955
effusive and crazy
over a high grade,

01:24:37.956 --> 01:24:39.070
but what we're saying is,

01:24:39.071 --> 01:24:41.590
oh it's really it's for
learning I care about,

01:24:41.591 --> 01:24:43.560
they're more apt to follow sort

01:24:43.561 --> 01:24:46.780
of they know that what
we really mean is this.

01:24:46.781 --> 01:24:49.845
Oh, I had a mother
recently ask me,

01:24:49.846 --> 01:24:52.390
my kids, they just
won't read for fun.

01:24:52.391 --> 01:24:54.095
My son will not read for fun.

01:24:54.096 --> 01:24:56.280
Number one, could you
give me a list of really

01:24:56.281 --> 01:24:59.020
challenging books that he
will want to read for fun?

01:24:59.021 --> 01:25:00.030
Could you do that for me?

01:25:00.031 --> 01:25:01.360
And I was like, that's like

01:25:01.361 --> 01:25:02.580
a magic list that

01:25:02.581 --> 01:25:04.225
if you could come up
with that would be great.

01:25:04.226 --> 01:25:06.800
And I said, well I have
to start here first,

01:25:06.801 --> 01:25:09.170
do your children
see you read for

01:25:09.171 --> 01:25:11.850
fun? And the answer is no.

01:25:11.851 --> 01:25:13.255
She had to admit that she was,

01:25:13.256 --> 01:25:15.830
I'm so busy ba ba ba blah,

01:25:15.831 --> 01:25:18.500
and when she did read she
read on a tablet and her kids

01:25:18.501 --> 01:25:21.270
didn't know what she
was doing on the tablet.

01:25:21.271 --> 01:25:23.500
It wasn't clear that
she was reading.

01:25:23.501 --> 01:25:25.955
So if you're not

01:25:25.956 --> 01:25:27.910
reading I don't really
know how I'm supposed to

01:25:27.911 --> 01:25:29.380
help you make it clear to

01:25:29.381 --> 01:25:30.440
your children that
you think it's

01:25:30.441 --> 01:25:31.705
an important thing to do.

01:25:31.706 --> 01:25:33.540
Then and I said, okay,

01:25:33.541 --> 01:25:35.225
well let's get back
to this magic list.

01:25:35.226 --> 01:25:36.920
What do your kids like to read?

01:25:36.921 --> 01:25:39.915
And she said, well
they really like,

01:25:39.916 --> 01:25:41.450
this one really
likes those Diary

01:25:41.451 --> 01:25:42.915
of a Wimpy Kid books but I threw

01:25:42.916 --> 01:25:47.310
those away because
they're stupid. I know.

01:25:47.570 --> 01:25:52.185
For the record, when
it comes to literacy,

01:25:52.186 --> 01:25:56.495
the ideal mix of
challenge levels

01:25:56.496 --> 01:26:02.175
is 30 percent comfortably
below your reading level,

01:26:02.176 --> 01:26:05.145
30 percent at
your reading level,

01:26:05.146 --> 01:26:07.160
I should say a
third, a third and a

01:26:07.161 --> 01:26:09.210
third above your reading level.

01:26:09.211 --> 01:26:11.790
This is about acquiring
new vocabulary.

01:26:11.791 --> 01:26:14.415
This is about strengthening it.

01:26:14.416 --> 01:26:16.670
This is about
comfort and fluency.

01:26:16.671 --> 01:26:19.950
This is about feeling
confident, competent feeling.

01:26:19.951 --> 01:26:21.860
It's the reason why
I go back and read

01:26:21.861 --> 01:26:24.100
things like "Little Women"
or "Pride and Prejudice".

01:26:24.101 --> 01:26:26.930
It's the reason kids love
repetitive shows because

01:26:26.931 --> 01:26:29.990
they take like "Blues Clues"
always happens the same.

01:26:29.991 --> 01:26:34.310
I mean, it drives us crazy
but for them it makes them so,

01:26:34.311 --> 01:26:36.670
it makes them feel
like they have mastery,

01:26:36.671 --> 01:26:38.810
because they know
what's coming next,

01:26:38.811 --> 01:26:41.130
and they can predict what
the answer is going to be,

01:26:41.131 --> 01:26:43.460
and that makes them
feel like they're smart,

01:26:43.461 --> 01:26:44.920
it makes them feel
like they're competent,

01:26:44.921 --> 01:26:47.440
it makes them feel
like they're powerful.

01:26:47.441 --> 01:26:48.880
And that's what
reading stuff that's

01:26:48.881 --> 01:26:50.545
below their reading level does.

01:26:50.546 --> 01:26:51.870
If your kid is in

01:26:51.871 --> 01:26:54.840
high school and every once
in a while likes to go back

01:26:54.841 --> 01:26:56.830
to those copies of
whatever that they

01:26:56.831 --> 01:26:59.415
read in fifth grade,
that's great.

01:26:59.416 --> 01:27:02.510
That is a really productive use
of their time, not,

01:27:02.511 --> 01:27:06.395
you know sweetie, I know
you're better than those books.

01:27:06.396 --> 01:27:08.330
What you're saying
to them is I think,

01:27:08.331 --> 01:27:10.110
not just that I think
what you love is

01:27:10.111 --> 01:27:13.005
stupid but I think
you're stupid.

01:27:13.006 --> 01:27:15.760
These choices you're
making are stupid thereby,

01:27:15.761 --> 01:27:17.800
I don't think as much of you.

01:27:17.801 --> 01:27:20.990
So there's like
16 different things all in

01:27:20.991 --> 01:27:24.195
one answer, but honestly,

01:27:24.196 --> 01:27:26.815
focusing more on those sort
of the long-term, the goals,

01:27:26.816 --> 01:27:29.120
the levers, making sure

01:27:29.121 --> 01:27:32.195
your kid knows you're
interested in him, her.

01:27:32.196 --> 01:27:33.660
Making sure your kid
knows that you

01:27:33.661 --> 01:27:34.880
really do care
about the learning,

01:27:34.881 --> 01:27:36.280
which means if
you're modeling that,

01:27:36.281 --> 01:27:38.535
you're going to have
to put yourself now,

01:27:38.536 --> 01:27:41.750
and like try things that
are uncomfortable for you.

01:27:41.751 --> 01:27:44.940
They're going to have to
see you trying some things

01:27:44.941 --> 01:27:48.600
that push you a little bit
beyond your ability level.

01:27:48.601 --> 01:27:50.460
Because we can say
that all we want,

01:27:50.461 --> 01:27:51.865
but until they see us take

01:27:51.866 --> 01:27:53.660
intellectual and
emotional risks,

01:27:53.661 --> 01:27:54.810
they don't really
believe that that's

01:27:54.811 --> 01:27:57.050
something worth doing for them.

01:27:57.660 --> 01:27:59.970
That's why it's so
important for teachers to

01:27:59.971 --> 01:28:01.340
admit when they don't
know the answers,

01:28:01.341 --> 01:28:02.580
and to say, you know what,

01:28:02.581 --> 01:28:04.185
I don't know, let me
look that up for you.

01:28:04.186 --> 01:28:06.540
Thank you so so much
for being here today.

01:28:06.541 --> 01:28:08.880
I really appreciate
it so much and I'm

01:28:08.881 --> 01:28:09.850
going to stick around and answer

01:28:09.851 --> 01:28:11.700
questions if anyone has more.

01:28:11.701 --> 01:28:13.220
If you didn't get
a book marker or

01:28:13.221 --> 01:28:14.995
a card or you want to...

01:28:14.996 --> 01:28:16.630
If you want to get
in touch with me,

01:28:16.631 --> 01:28:18.640
the contact form at my website,

01:28:18.641 --> 01:28:20.050
it's just my email,

01:28:20.051 --> 01:28:23.870
go straight to my email box
and I'll be ha...

01:28:23.871 --> 01:28:25.390
And for those of you who

01:28:25.391 --> 01:28:27.600
want a spouse to sort
of hear some of this,

01:28:27.601 --> 01:28:30.325
because that's a question
I get a lot too,

01:28:30.326 --> 01:28:33.700
I will be speaking
on Mercer Island

01:28:33.701 --> 01:28:36.400
tomorrow night at
the Mercer Island High School

01:28:36.401 --> 01:28:37.800
